    prov:wasAttributedTo <http://example.org/rdodi/agent/YusufAltunel> .

chx:Document a doc:ReportSection ; rdfs:label "Keys and addresses: chapter 4 of the 3rd edition recomputed from the standards and cross-checked against Bitcoin Core 31.1"@en ; skos:definition "This document renews chapter 4 of the 3rd edition by deriving every key, commitment, script and address of the chapter from its own primary standards with a readable toolkit, and by comparing the result with Bitcoin Core 31.1 and with the reference implementation that the book itself runs, so that each number the chapter prints is explained, reproduced, and reported where the book and its sources disagree." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04/research> ;
    doc:hasSection chx:S_Keys, chx:S_Format, chx:S_Address, chx:S_Practice, chx:S_Semantics .
chx:S_Keys a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Keys"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Keys" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 1 ;
    skos:definition "This branch of the chapter starts from a payment. Alice wants to pay Bob, but the thousands of full nodes that will verify her transaction do not know who Alice or Bob are, and the design is meant to keep it that way, to protect their privacy (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Alice therefore has to tell the network that Bob should receive some of her bitcoins without tying any part of the transaction to Bob's real-world identity, and without linking it to the other payments Bob receives. The method must also guarantee that only Bob can spend what he receives afterwards. Keys are the answer to both demands, and the rest of the chapter is the story of how a key becomes something that one person can hand to another.\n\nWithout keys there would be no way to decide who may spend a coin. A bank answers that question by looking up an account holder in its own records, but a network of nodes that do not trust one another has no such record and no such authority. Bitcoin replaces the account holder with a pair of numbers: whoever can produce a valid signature with the right private number may spend, and anyone can check the signature with the matching public number. The original Bitcoin paper describes this in its simplest form, in which a receiver like Bob accepts bitcoins to a public key in a transaction that the spender signs, and in which full nodes verify that the spender's signature commits to the output of a hash function that itself commits to Bob's public key and the other transaction details (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nYou meet keys every time you use a wallet. Chapter 1 described the wallet as the software through which people use Bitcoin and explained that in a noncustodial wallet only the user holds the keys. Chapter 2 showed that a transaction input carries a digital signature and an output receives funds. This chapter opens those two ideas up: it shows what the signature is made from, how the number that stands behind a payment is generated, and why the strings that people actually copy, such as an address, look the way they do.\n\nThe branch is arranged as the book arranges its argument. The key pair section introduces public key cryptography, the digital signature, the private key and the public key. The randomness section explains where a private key must come from. The curve section describes the mathematics that turns a private key into a public key, and the hash function section describes the second tool, which lets a long public key be replaced by a short commitment. Later branches of the chapter combine these ingredients into the addresses that modern Bitcoin software uses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nTwo warnings run through everything that follows. The private key must stay secret at all times, because revealing it to a third party is equivalent to giving that party control over the bitcoins it secures. It must also be backed up and protected from accidental loss, because a lost private key cannot be recovered and the funds it secured are lost for good (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Secrecy and survival are the whole of key management." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Keys> .
chx:S_KeyPair a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Key pair"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Key pair" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 2 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Keys ;
    skos:definition "A key pair is the combination of a private key and a public key that belongs to it. In Bitcoin the key pair is what controls access to bitcoins: the public key is used to receive funds, and the private key is used to sign transactions that spend them (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The two keys are not independent. The public key is calculated from the private key, and the mathematical relationship between them is what allows the private key to produce signatures that anyone can validate against the public key without learning the private key.\n\nThe pair is the smallest unit of ownership that Bitcoin knows. A wallet, in the words of the book, contains a collection of key pairs, each consisting of a private key and a public key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Every question about who may spend a given output reduces to the question of who holds the private key of the pair that the output was paid to, which is why this second level of the branch deserves careful reading.\n\nThe pair works in one direction only, and everything else follows from that. The private key is simply a very large whole number, chosen at random; the public key is obtained by adding a fixed point on the curve to itself that many times, an operation the next level of the chapter sets out in full. Computing forward is cheap, because doubling and adding reach any multiple in a few hundred steps rather than in as many steps as the number is large. Computing backward - recovering the multiplier from the result - has no comparable shortcut, and that asymmetry is the whole of the protection. It also explains the book's tip that a wallet need store only the private key: the public key is not a second secret to be guarded but a value that can be recalculated from the first whenever it is wanted (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets the pair wherever a wallet is created or a key is moved. A new wallet reports the keys it has made rather than their values; an export writes the private key out in a text form and leaves the public key to be recomputed; a block explorer shows the public side, or a commitment to it, because that is the half which has to travel. The pair is met again in every later chapter of this course: the signature of chapter 2 is made with the private half and checked with the public half, and the tree of keys in chapter 5 is a way of making very many pairs from one secret (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nTwo cautions. The names mislead a little: a public key is public only once it has been used, and a wallet that hands out a fresh commitment for each payment is deliberately keeping it back, for reasons the section on address reuse gives. And the pair is not an identity. It proves that whoever signs holds the private key; it says nothing about who that is, nor about whether the key is still in the hands it was in yesterday. Treating a key as a person is the mistake behind most of the losses this chapter's later sections warn about (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#KeyPair> .
chx:S_PublicKeyCryptography a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Public key cryptography"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Public key cryptography" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 3 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_KeyPair ;
    skos:definition "Public key cryptography, also called asymmetric cryptography, is a branch of cryptography in which every participant has two different but mathematically related keys. The book dates its invention to the 1970s and calls it a mathematical foundation for modern computer and information security (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Several suitable mathematical functions have been found since, among them the exponentiation of prime numbers and the multiplication of points on an elliptic curve. What they share is that they are easy to calculate in one direction and infeasible to calculate in the opposite direction with the computers and algorithms available today, and Bitcoin uses elliptic curve addition and multiplication as the basis of its cryptography (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reason Bitcoin needs the technique is easy to misjudge. It is not used to encrypt, that is to make secret, the transactions: everything on the blockchain is public. Its useful property is the ability to generate digital signatures. A private key applied to a transaction produces a numerical signature that only someone who knows the private key can produce, yet anyone who has the public key and the transaction can verify it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). That is precisely what a network of strangers needs, because it makes it possible for everyone to verify every signature on every transaction while only the owners of private keys can create valid ones.\n\nThe technique is met wherever people need to prove something to others who do not trust them, and Chapter 3 has already shown one case: the signatures on the release files that a Bitcoin Core user checks before running the software. In Bitcoin itself the technique appears in two places. The spender's signature in a transaction input is checked against a public key, and the public key itself is hidden behind an address until the moment it is spent, as later sections of this chapter explain.\n\nThe mechanism has three steps. A private key, which is only a number, is chosen at random. A one-way function, elliptic curve multiplication in Bitcoin, turns it into the public key. When a transaction must be authorised, the private key is used to compute a signature over the transaction, and a verifier combines the signature, the transaction and the public key to decide whether the signature is genuine. The private key itself never leaves the signer's hands and is not revealed by the signature (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bitcoin has traditionally used the ECDSA signature scheme over the secp256k1 curve with SHA256 hashes, and a newer scheme, Schnorr signatures, is standardised for the same curve (Wuille et al., 2020).\n\nThree misunderstandings are common. The first is that signing hides the transaction, which it does not. The second is that the public key could be used to recover the private key, which the one-way property rules out for the computers and algorithms of today (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The third is that the one-way property has been proved: the book says only that the reverse calculation is infeasible with today's computers and algorithms (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), which is a statement about the present state of knowledge and not a mathematical theorem." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#PublicKeyCryptography> .
chx:S_DigitalSignature a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Digital signature"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Digital signature" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 4 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_KeyPair ;
    skos:definition "A digital signature is a number that a signer computes from a message and from a private key. Anyone who has the message, the signature and the matching public key can verify the signature, that is, can check that it was produced by the holder of the private key and that the message has not been altered since. The book describes this as the central benefit of asymmetric cryptography for Bitcoin: the signature can only be produced by someone with knowledge of the private key, yet it can be verified by anyone with the public key and the transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe signature is how Bitcoin answers the question of who may spend. A transaction input does not carry a name or a password; it carries a signature that proves that the spender controls the key to which the earlier output was paid, as Chapter 2 already noted. Because verification needs only public information, every full node can check every signature independently, which is what lets thousands of nodes that do not know each other agree on which spends are valid.\n\nIn Bitcoin the signature is used in every spend of an output that is locked to a key. In the earliest form of payment, which later sections describe, the output script asks for a signature that matches a public key, and the check is carried out by a single operation of the script language. Outside Bitcoin, the same mechanism signs documents, software and web certificates.\n\nThe scheme that Bitcoin has traditionally used is the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm, ECDSA, over the secp256k1 curve with SHA256 hashes, and a signature is encoded in a variable-size format of up to 72 bytes (Wuille et al., 2020). The chapter's toolkit contains a teaching sketch of the algorithm: given a private key, a message hash and a nonce, which is a one-time secret number, it produces a pair of numbers, and a verifier that knows only the public key accepts the pair for that message hash. The same pair is rejected for a message hash that differs in a single unit, which is the property that ties a signature to one particular transaction. Bitcoin's newer Schnorr signatures use a fixed 64-byte format and a 32-byte public key (Wuille et al., 2020).\n\nA signature scheme is only as good as its handling of the one-time number that goes into every signature. The sketch in the toolkit takes the nonce as an argument so that its output is reproducible; a real signer must derive it deterministically or draw it from a secure random source, and the toolkit says plainly that it is not for use with real funds. ECDSA signatures are also malleable: if a pair of numbers is a valid signature, then the pair with the second number replaced by the group order minus that number is also valid for the same key and message, which Bitcoin handles with a policy rule on the network (Wuille et al., 2020). One of the stated motivations of the segregated witness upgrade, described in its own section, is that signature data no longer takes part in the transaction identifier, so that changes to how a transaction was signed no longer change its identity (Lombrozo et al., 2015)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#DigitalSignature> .
chx:S_PrivateKey a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Private key"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Private key" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 5 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_KeyPair ;
    skos:definition "A private key is simply a number, picked at random. Control over the private key is the root of user control over all funds associated with the corresponding public key: the key is used to create the signatures that spend bitcoins by proving control of the funds used in a transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's example private key, written in hexadecimal, is 1E99423A4ED27608A15A2616A2B0E9E52CED330AC530EDCC32C8FFC6A526AEDD, which is 256 bits shown as 64 hexadecimal digits of 4 bits each (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nEverything else in the system is derived from this one number. The public key, the addresses and the signatures follow from it, while it cannot be recovered from them. That asymmetry makes the private key both the most valuable and the most dangerous secret a Bitcoin user has: whoever learns it can spend the funds, and whoever loses it cannot get them back. The book states both consequences directly, that revealing the key is equivalent to giving control to a third party and that a lost key cannot be recovered (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe private key is met in wallet software, usually out of sight. Chapter 1 explained that a wallet may derive its keys from a recovery code, and later sections of this chapter show the private key written in other formats, such as the wallet import format used to move a key between wallets. A simple tip in the book makes the nature of the key tangible: tossing a coin 256 times gives the binary digits of a random private key that could be used in a Bitcoin wallet (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nNot every 256-bit number is a valid key. The private key can be any number between 0 and n minus 1, where n is a constant slightly less than 2 to the 256, defined as the order of the elliptic curve that Bitcoin uses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The constant is FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFEBAAEDCE6AF48A03BBFD25E8CD0364141 in hexadecimal (Certicom Research, 2010). Software therefore picks a random 256-bit number and checks that it is less than n, and tries again otherwise. The chance of having to retry is the share of 256-bit numbers that are not below n, about 3.7 times 10 to the minus 39. A key of zero would be useless, because zero times the generator point is the point at infinity and not a public key, so a usable key lies between 1 and n minus 1.\n\nTwo details deserve attention. The book prints n as 1.1578 times 10 to the 77, but computing the constant from the standard gives 1.1579 to five significant figures; the difference is a slip in the book's rounding and has no practical consequence. The second detail matters a great deal: any process that is less than completely random can significantly reduce the security of the key and of the bitcoins it controls (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The key space is enormous, about 10 to the 77 numbers, compared with the roughly 10 to the 80 atoms that the book mentions for the visible universe, but that size protects only a key that was chosen without bias." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#PrivateKey> .
chx:S_PublicKey a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Public key"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Public key" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 6 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_KeyPair ;
    skos:definition "A public key is the number, in fact a point on an elliptic curve, that is calculated from a private key and can be shared with anyone. In the book's notation the private key is k, the public key is K, and the relation is K = k times G, where G is a constant point called the generator point (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For the book's example private key the public key is the point whose two coordinates are x = F028892BAD7ED57D2FB57BF33081D5CFCF6F9ED3D3D7F159C2E2FFF579DC341A and y = 07CF33DA18BD734C600B96A72BBC4749D5141C90EC8AC328AE52DDFE2E505BDB (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe public key is what lets the rest of the world check a signature without being able to forge one. Because the generator point is the same for every Bitcoin user, a given private key always produces the same public key, and because the calculation cannot be run backwards, the public key does not reveal the private key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book summarises this in a tip: a private key can be converted into a public key, but a public key cannot be converted back into a private key, because the math only works one way.\n\nIn the earliest design a public key was placed directly in an output script, and Bob would hand his public key to Alice. The book points out that this is impractical, since the key is long: imagine Bob reading the two 64-digit coordinates to Alice over the phone (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Most of the later sections of this chapter, compressed keys, hashed commitments and addresses, exist to solve exactly that practical problem.\n\nThe calculation is an elliptic curve multiplication, described in its own section. Software computes K from k by repeated doubling and adding of points, and many Bitcoin implementations delegate the work to the libsecp256k1 library (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The result is a pair of 256-bit coordinates, which can be written in several encodings. The chapter's toolkit reproduces the book's coordinates from the book's private key, and it confirms that the point satisfies the curve equation. The parity of y, odd for the example key, decides whether the compressed encoding starts with 02 or 03, as the compressed key section explains.\n\nA public key is public only in the sense that it can be shared, not in the sense that it must be published early. Bitcoin addresses hide the public key behind a hash until the key is used to spend, and the book's later sections explain what is gained by that. Do not confuse the public key with the address: they are related, but the address is derived from the public key by further steps and has its own formats." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#PublicKey> .
chx:S_RandomSource a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Randomness"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Randomness" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 7 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Keys ;
    skos:definition "Randomness is the property of a number or a sequence of numbers that nobody can predict or repeat. The first and most important step in generating keys is to find a secure source of it, which computer scientists call entropy (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The word describes both the quality that is needed and, loosely, the material from which random numbers are made.\n\nA private key protects funds only to the extent that an attacker cannot guess it. A key chosen from a source that is predictable, repeatable or biased may be guessed in far fewer attempts than the size of the key space suggests, even though the key itself looks like an arbitrary 64-digit number. For that reason the source of the number matters more than the number.\n\nSecure randomness works by gathering unpredictability from the physical world and then stretching it. The operating system collects events whose exact timing nobody can reproduce - the intervals between interrupts, the arrival of network packets, the noise of a dedicated hardware source - and mixes them into a pool. A program then asks the system for bytes, and the system returns the output of a cryptographic generator seeded from that pool, which is indistinguishable from random to anyone who does not know the seed. Two consequences matter for a learner. The amount of genuine unpredictability is a property of the seed, not of the length of the output, so stretching 128 bits of entropy into 256 bits of key leaves 128 bits of real strength. And because the quality lives in the system rather than in the arithmetic, the book's rule - use the cryptographically secure generator and do not write your own - is advice about which door to knock on rather than about which formula to use (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets the distinction in the standard library of almost any language, which offers two generators side by side: one made for simulations, fast and deliberately reproducible from a seed, and one made for secrets, which cannot be reseeded by the caller and so cannot be replayed. Python names them in just that way, and its documentation says outright that the reproducible one must not be used for security (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The same distinction appears in this chapter's own visual for the concept, where four thousand keys drawn from the reproducible generator are sorted by their first digit: the bars are level, which is what a usable generator looks like, and the picture is identical on every build because the draw was seeded on purpose.\n\nThe failures here are historical rather than hypothetical, and they share a shape: the arithmetic was correct and the source was not. A generator seeded from the clock, a generator that restarts from the same state after a reboot, a generator whose output is derived from a passphrase a person invented - each produces keys that look like arbitrary sixty-four-digit numbers and that an attacker can search in a tiny fraction of the key space. A learner should take from this that the size of the key space is an upper bound on security and never a measurement of it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#RandomSource> .
chx:S_Entropy a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Entropy"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Entropy" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 8 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RandomSource ;
    skos:definition "Entropy, in the sense the book uses the word, is the unpredictability of the material from which a random number is made, and it is counted in bits. Each fair coin toss that nobody can foresee contributes one bit, so the book's tip that 256 tosses give the binary digits of a random private key is a statement about 256 bits of entropy (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The phrase 'cryptographically secure random number generators to produce 256 bits of entropy' describes the practice of Bitcoin software (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe count of bits is the count of equally likely possibilities an attacker would have to try. A source with 256 bits of entropy offers 2 to the 256 possibilities, a number with 78 decimal digits and about 10 to the 77 in size, as the book puts it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). If a program claims to produce a 256-bit key but draws it from a source with only 32 bits of entropy, an attacker faces about four billion possibilities and not 10 to the 77, and the key is weak whatever it looks like.\n\nEntropy is met under several names. Chapter 1 explained that a wallet's recovery code is the basis of its keys and gave the initial entropy of the standard recovery words as 128 to 256 bits, and the Python documentation points to the same idea when it says that the secrets module provides access to the most secure source of randomness that the operating system provides (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nEntropy is not generated by a clever formula. A formula can only stretch entropy that already exists, which is what a pseudorandom number generator does when it expands a short seed into a long stream. Therefore the question for any key generator is where its seed comes from. The book's recipe collects a larger string of random bits from a cryptographically secure source and feeds it to SHA256, which conveniently produces a 256-bit value that can be read as a number; if the number is below n it is a suitable private key, and otherwise the process repeats with fresh randomness (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nHigh-quality-looking output does not prove high entropy. A number that passes casual inspection can come from a seed as small as the current time, and such a key can be found by an attacker who simply tries all plausible seeds, a search of a few million tries rather than the 10 to the 77 that the size of the key space suggests. Nor can the amount of entropy in a number be measured by looking at the number: two keys that look equally arbitrary may have been drawn from sources that differ by seventy orders of magnitude. Treat entropy as a property of the source and never of the printed result, and ask of any key generator where its seed came from." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Entropy> .
chx:S_SecureRandomness a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Secure randomness"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Secure randomness" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 9 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RandomSource ;
    skos:definition "Secure randomness is what a cryptographically secure pseudorandom number generator, abbreviated CSPRNG, delivers: numbers that cannot be predicted by an attacker even when the attacker has seen earlier output. The book's warning on the subject is categorical. Do not write your own code to create a random number and do not use a simple random number generator offered by your programming language; use a CSPRNG with a seed from a source of sufficient entropy, and study the documentation of the library you choose to make sure that it is cryptographically secure, because correct implementation of the CSPRNG is critical to the security of the keys (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe warning exists because ordinary generators are designed for a different job. The Python documentation says that the default generator of its random module is designed for modelling and simulation, not for security or cryptography (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Such a generator is a fixed procedure that starts from a seed, so anyone who learns or guesses the seed can reproduce every number that follows. For a private key that is fatal: a key is only as secret as its generator is unpredictable.\n\nEvery serious programming environment offers both kinds of generator, and the difference is visible in their documentation. The documentation of Python's secrets module says that the module is used for generating cryptographically strong random numbers suitable for managing data such as passwords, account authentication and security tokens, and that it should be used in preference to the default generator of the random module, which is designed for modelling and simulation, not security or cryptography (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nA CSPRNG gathers entropy from the operating system, mixes it and expands it into as many unpredictable bits as are asked for. In Python the call secrets.randbelow(m) returns a random integer from 0 up to but not including m, drawn from the most secure source the operating system provides (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Choosing a key with it takes one line: ask for a number below n minus 1 and add 1, which yields a number from 1 to n minus 1. The default generator of the random module, by contrast, is repeatable on purpose: two generators started from the same seed give the same number, a property that the chapter's checks confirm and that is useful for experiments and disastrous for keys.\n\nFollowing the rule requires attention to details that are easy to overlook. A generator that is secure in principle can be misused by seeding it with a poor value, by calling it before the system has gathered enough entropy, or by running it on a machine whose random source is compromised. Keys should be generated on a device that you control, and the quality of the software should not be taken on trust. The book names generating code that is compromised with a back door as one of the pitfalls of paper wallets, a subject that a later section returns to (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#SecureRandomness> .
chx:S_Curve a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "The curve"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The curve" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 10 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Keys ;
    skos:definition "The curve is the mathematical object on which Bitcoin's public key cryptography is built. Elliptic curve cryptography, abbreviated ECC, is a type of asymmetric or public key cryptography based on the discrete logarithm problem as expressed by addition and multiplication on the points of an elliptic curve (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This level of the chapter gathers everything that is needed to understand that sentence: the arithmetic of a finite field, the curve itself, the way points are added, the particular curve that Bitcoin uses and the standard that defines it, the generator point, multiplication of a point by a number, and the problem that makes the whole construction hard to reverse.\n\nA reader who only wants to use Bitcoin can treat the conversion from private key to public key as a black box, but a reader who wants to judge the security of Bitcoin cannot. Every claim of the form 'the public key does not reveal the private key' rests on the properties of this one curve. The book therefore spends a section on it, and it notes that the math is identical whether the curve is drawn over the real numbers, shown over a small finite field or taken over the enormous field that Bitcoin uses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe construction works in three layers, each built from the one below. The bottom layer is a finite field: the numbers are the remainders after division by one very large prime, and addition and multiplication are the ordinary operations followed by taking the remainder, which keeps every result inside the same finite set. The middle layer is the curve, the set of pairs in that field satisfying the curve's equation, together with one extra point that acts as a zero. On that set an addition of points is defined geometrically - draw the line through two points, take the third point where it meets the curve, reflect it - and the definition turns the curve into a group, which is the only property the cryptography needs. The top layer is multiplication of a point by a whole number, which is repeated addition carried out by doubling, so that a multiplier of two hundred and fifty-six bits costs a few hundred operations rather than astronomically many. A public key is the generator point multiplied by the private key, and the discrete logarithm problem is the absence of any comparable shortcut in the other direction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Certicom Research, 2010).\n\nThe reader meets this level twice over, at two scales. The small scale is the chapter's own worked examples over the field with seventeen elements, where the whole curve has few enough points to list and a student can check an addition on paper; the arithmetic there is identical to the arithmetic Bitcoin uses, which is the point of showing it. The large scale is the curve named in the standard, whose parameters this chapter quotes from the specification and whose generator point it encodes; the same parameters are what the library inside every wallet is compiled with (Certicom Research, 2010) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nTwo cautions about pictures and about proofs. The smooth curve drawn in textbooks over the real numbers is a teaching aid: over a finite field the points are a scatter with no shape to the eye, and a learner who reasons from the drawing will reach wrong conclusions about how many points lie near one another. And the hardness of the discrete logarithm problem is not a theorem but the present state of the art; no proof exists that no shortcut can be found, so every statement about the strength of a key in this chapter is a statement about what is known today (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Curve> .
chx:S_FiniteField a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Finite field"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Finite field" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 11 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Curve ;
    skos:definition "A finite field of prime order p, written F_p, is the set of whole numbers from 0 to p minus 1 together with the four arithmetic operations, in which every result is replaced by its remainder after division by p. The book writes the remainder operation as 'mod p' and says that it indicates that the curve is over a finite field of prime order p (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Because the remainder always lies between 0 and p minus 1, the numbers never grow beyond the size of p, and every non-zero number has a partner by which it can be multiplied to give 1.\n\nThe elliptic curve of Bitcoin is defined over a finite field instead of over the real numbers, and this choice is what makes it usable in software. Real numbers have to be rounded by a computer, whereas arithmetic modulo a prime is exact. The price is that the curve no longer looks like a smooth line. The book remarks that because the curve is defined over a finite field of prime order, it looks like a pattern of dots scattered in two dimensions, which makes it difficult to visualise, although the math is identical to that of an elliptic curve over real numbers (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nModular arithmetic is met outside cryptography as well, in clocks, in check digits and in the checksums of this chapter's later sections. In Bitcoin it appears whenever a coordinate of a point is calculated, and the prime in question is p = 2 to the 256 minus 2 to the 32 minus 2 to the 9 minus 2 to the 8 minus 2 to the 7 minus 2 to the 6 minus 2 to the 4 minus 1, a very large prime number (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The standard gives the same prime in hexadecimal, as FFFFFFFF followed by repeated groups of F and ending in FFFFFC2F, and in the same long form (Certicom Research, 2010). Written more briefly it is 2 to the 256 minus 2 to the 32 minus 977, an equality that the chapter's checks verify.\n\nIn Python the operator % gives the remainder, and pow(a, -1, p) gives the number by which a must be multiplied, modulo p, to obtain 1. In the field with 17 elements the partner of 5 is 7, because 5 times 7 is 35 and 35 leaves the remainder 1 when divided by 17; the chapter's checks confirm that every number from 1 to 16 has such a partner. Division by a is defined as multiplication by its partner. That operation, the modular inverse, is what the slope calculation in point addition needs, and the toolkit of this chapter uses exactly this call to add points on the real curve.\n\nA finite field is not a number line, so ordinary intuitions about size and order fail: 16 is not 'greater' than 1 in any useful sense once results wrap around. The prime matters, too. A probabilistic primality test confirms that Bitcoin's p is prime, and with a modulus that is not prime some numbers would have no inverse. Finally, remember that p is only slightly smaller than 2 to the 256, the size of a private key, but it is a different number from n, the order of the curve; the two are easily confused." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#FiniteField> .
chx:S_EllipticCurve a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Elliptic curve"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Elliptic curve" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 12 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Curve ;
    skos:definition "An elliptic curve is the set of all points (x, y) whose coordinates satisfy an equation of a particular form, together with one extra point called the point at infinity. The curve of Bitcoin has the equation y squared = x cubed + 7, which the book writes as y squared mod p = (x cubed + 7) mod p because the arithmetic is carried out in a finite field (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the notation of the standard the equation is y squared = x cubed + ax + b, and for this curve a is 0 and b is 7 (Certicom Research, 2010).\n\nThe reason for choosing such a curve is that its points can be added to one another by a simple rule, so that the points form a system on which multiplication by a whole number is easy to compute and its inverse is believed hard. Those two properties together give Bitcoin its one-way function. The curve is not valuable for its shape but for the algebra that its points obey.\n\nOver the real numbers the curve is a smooth line, drawn in the book as a figure that is symmetric about the x-axis, which is to say that it is reflected like a mirror by the axis (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Over a finite field it becomes a scatter of dots on a grid. The book shows the same equation over the field with 17 elements, and the chapter's checks find that it has exactly 17 points whose coordinates are whole numbers from 0 to 16, which together with the point at infinity makes 18. Bitcoin's own curve is, in the book's words, a much more complex pattern of dots on an unfathomably large grid (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nTo check that a point lies on the curve you substitute its coordinates into the equation. The book gives a point P with two 77-digit coordinates and shows in Python that x cubed plus 7 minus y squared leaves the remainder 0 when divided by p, so the point is on the curve (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); the chapter's checks reproduce that result. A point whose coordinates do not give 0 is not a valid public key. For every point (x, y) on the curve the point (x, minus y) is also on the curve, which is the symmetry about the x-axis in the finite world: in the field with 17 elements the point (1, 5) has the mirror image (1, 12), since 17 minus 5 is 12.\n\nThe word 'elliptic' is misleading, because the curve is not an ellipse. Do not take the smooth picture literally for Bitcoin, either: it explains the geometry of addition, but the real curve has only whole-number points modulo p and the lines of the drawing do not exist there. The book is careful on this point and says that the math is the same, which is why the drawing remains a faithful guide (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#EllipticCurve> .
chx:S_PointAddition a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Point addition and the point at infinity"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Point addition and the point at infinity" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 13 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Curve ;
    skos:definition "Point addition is the operation by which two points of an elliptic curve are combined into a third point of the same curve. Given two points P1 and P2, there is a third point P3 = P1 + P2, also on the curve; the book describes it geometrically: draw a line between P1 and P2, which will intersect the curve in exactly one additional place, call that point P3' = (x, y), and then reflect it in the x-axis to get P3 = (x, minus y) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The curve also contains the point at infinity, which roughly corresponds to the role of zero in addition.\n\nAddition is the only operation that has to be defined, because everything else in elliptic curve cryptography is built from it. Multiplication of a point is repeated addition, the public key is such a multiple, and the verification of a signature adds points. That the rule yields a third point of the curve, and not a point somewhere else in the plane, is what makes the curve a closed system. The book adds that the addition is associative, so that A + B + C can be written without parentheses and without ambiguity (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nIn the field with 17 elements the points of the curve y squared = x cubed + 7 can be added by hand. Adding (1, 5) and (2, 7) gives (1, 12). Adding (1, 5) to itself gives (2, 10). Adding (1, 5) and (1, 12), two points with the same x value and different y values, gives the point at infinity. Adding the point at infinity to any point gives that point back. The chapter's checks confirm each of these sums and also the associativity rule on an example; they use the same rules that the toolkit applies to the big curve.\n\nThere are special cases, and the book explains why they require the point at infinity. If P1 and P2 are the same point, the line between them is replaced by the tangent to the curve at that point, which intersects the curve in exactly one new point; the techniques of calculus find its slope, and they work even though the points have whole-number coordinates. If P1 and P2 have the same x value but different y values, the line is vertical and the result is the point at infinity. If P1 is the point at infinity, then P1 + P2 = P2, and the same holds with the roles exchanged, which shows how the point at infinity plays the role of zero (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In software the point at infinity is sometimes represented by x = y = 0, which does not satisfy the curve equation but is an easy separate case to check for (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe formulas that software uses are algebraic, not geometric, and they work in the finite field with modular division, so the picture of a line crossing a curve must not be taken literally. The chapter's toolkit shows the cost of care: each case, doubling, inverse points and the point at infinity, needs its own branch, and a mistake in one of them produces points that are not on the curve and public keys that nobody can use. This is one more reason to rely on a well-tested library in practice." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#PointAddition> .
chx:S_Secp256k1 a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The secp256k1 curve"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The secp256k1 curve" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 14 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Curve ;
    skos:definition "The name secp256k1 identifies the specific elliptic curve and the set of constants that Bitcoin uses. The curve is y squared = x cubed + 7 over a finite field of prime order p, with p = 2 to the 256 minus 2 to the 32 minus 2 to the 9 minus 2 to the 8 minus 2 to the 7 minus 2 to the 6 minus 2 to the 4 minus 1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The standard that defines it specifies the parameters as a sextuple of the prime p, the coefficients a and b, the base point G, the order n of G and the cofactor h, which the standard defines as the number of points of the curve divided by n; for secp256k1 the coefficient a is 0, the coefficient b is 7 and the cofactor is 1, which means that the points of the curve are exactly the multiples of G (Certicom Research, 2010).\n\nEvery Bitcoin public key is a point on this one curve, and every Bitcoin signature is checked against it. Fixing a single curve and a single generator point means that all participants perform the same calculation and obtain the same answer from the same private key. The choice also fixes the security level: the standard's table of properties gives secp256k1 a security strength of 128 bits for a field size of 256 bits (Certicom Research, 2010), and that figure, not the 256 bits of the key itself, is the amount of work an attacker is estimated to need.\n\nThe name is met in the documentation of every Bitcoin library, in the title of the Schnorr signature standard, which is called Schnorr Signatures for secp256k1 (Wuille et al., 2020), and in the book's remark that Bitcoin uses a specific curve and set of constants as defined in a standard called secp256k1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The name is also a small code. In the words of the standard, each name begins with sec to denote Standards for Efficient Cryptography, followed by p to denote parameters over a prime field, a number for the length in bits of the field size, a k for parameters associated with a Koblitz curve or an r for verifiably random parameters, and a sequence number (Certicom Research, 2010).\n\nTo use the curve in a program one needs only p, the generator G and the order n. The chapter's checks verify the book's example point P against the equation, compute p from its short form, and confirm that the generator point lies on the curve and that adding it to itself n times gives the point at infinity. They also parse the constants from the standard's text and compare them with the toolkit's constants, so that the numbers used in this chapter are the standard's and not copies from memory.\n\nOne statement in the book does not match its source. The book says that secp256k1 was established by the National Institute of Standards and Technology, but the curve is defined in SEC 2, Standards for Efficient Cryptography, published by Certicom Research in 2010, and the status table in that document marks secp256k1 with a dash in the NIST column, meaning not conformant with the NIST list, while secp256r1 is marked r, explicitly recommended (Certicom Research, 2010). The error is easy to repeat and matters when a reader is asked whether Bitcoin's curve was chosen by a government agency: according to the standard it is not one of the curves on the NIST list." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Secp256k1> .
chx:S_CurveStandard a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Curve standard (SEC 2 and NIST)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Curve standard (SEC 2 and NIST)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 15 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Curve ;
    skos:definition "A curve standard is a published document that fixes the constants of an elliptic curve so that independent programmers obtain the same curve. The standard behind Bitcoin is SEC 2, Recommended Elliptic Curve Domain Parameters, version 2.0, published on 27 January 2010 by Certicom Research under the name Standards for Efficient Cryptography (Certicom Research, 2010). It lists recommended parameters over prime fields in several sizes, from 192 to 521 bits, and gives each set of parameters a nickname such as secp256k1 or secp256r1.\n\nA curve is not safe merely because it has a name. Choosing the coefficients and the generator point is a decision that the user of the curve cannot check without help, which is why standards distinguish between parameters associated with a Koblitz curve, marked k, and parameters chosen verifiably at random, marked r (Certicom Research, 2010). The standard also records how each set of parameters aligns with other standards, which tells a reader who else supports the curve and who recommends it.\n\nThe alignment table is Table 2 of the document. Its columns name other standards: ANSI X9.62, ANSI X9.63, echeck, IEEE P1363, IPSec, NIST and WAP. A dash denotes parameters not conformant with the standard of that column, the letter c denotes conformant parameters and the letter r denotes parameters explicitly recommended in the standard (Certicom Research, 2010). In the NIST column secp256k1 has a dash and secp256r1 has an r, which is the evidence for the correction in the previous concept. The chapter's checks read the table rows from the text of the standard.\n\nTo trace a curve to its source, one starts from the name and looks for its section in the standard. Section 2.4.1 specifies secp256k1 and section 2.4.2 specifies secp256r1 (Certicom Research, 2010). The parameters appear as hexadecimal numbers, and the prime p is also given as 2 to the 256 minus 2 to the 32 minus 2 to the 9 minus 2 to the 8 minus 2 to the 7 minus 2 to the 6 minus 2 to the 4 minus 1, the form the book uses. Anyone implementing the curve can check a copy of the constants against this text.\n\nA standards document is a draft or a version at a given date, and the table reflects the status at that date. SEC 2 version 2.0 labels itself a draft in its page headers, so statements about 'the NIST list' should be read as statements about the list that the document refers to. When a textbook and a primary document disagree, the primary document is the better evidence for what it says itself, and the discrepancy is worth reporting rather than silently correcting." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#CurveStandard> .
chx:S_GeneratorPoint a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Generator point"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Generator point" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 16 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Curve ;
    skos:definition "The generator point, written G, is the predetermined point on the curve by which a private key is multiplied to give the public key. It is specified as part of the secp256k1 standard and is always the same for all keys in Bitcoin (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The standard gives it in compressed form as 02 followed by the x coordinate 79BE667EF9DCBBAC55A06295CE870B07029BFCDB2DCE28D959F2815B16F81798, and in uncompressed form as 04 followed by that x coordinate and a y coordinate of 256 bits (Certicom Research, 2010).\n\nBecause the generator point is the same for every Bitcoin user, a private key k multiplied by G will always result in the same public key K (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). That uniqueness lets any two programs agree on the public key without exchanging any information about it. The same property is what lets this chapter's toolkit reproduce the public key that the book prints for its example private key.\n\nThe generator appears wherever a public key is derived from a private key and wherever a signature is verified. The book pictures the process as starting from G and doubling it: its figure shows G, 2G and 4G as a geometric operation on the curve (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the standard, G is paired with n, the order of G, and a cofactor h of 1, the ratio between the number of points of the curve and n, which says that the curve holds no points besides the multiples of G (Certicom Research, 2010).\n\nThe order n is the number of times G must be added to itself to arrive at the point at infinity. The chapter's checks confirm for the standard's n that n times G is the point at infinity, that n minus 1 times G is the mirror image of G, and that n is prime. The first of these facts means that private keys beyond n minus 1 would only repeat earlier public keys, which is why the valid private keys are the numbers from 1 to n minus 1. The second says that the multiples of G form a loop of exactly n members, of which the public keys are the members other than the point at infinity.\n\nThe generator is public and standard, so it contributes nothing to secrecy; the secrecy is entirely in the multiplier. A reader should also not confuse the generator point G with the base point of a different curve, nor with a public key: G is a constant of the standard, while a public key is a multiple of G chosen by a private key." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#GeneratorPoint> .
chx:S_PointMultiplication a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Elliptic curve multiplication"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Elliptic curve multiplication" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 17 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Curve ;
    skos:definition "Elliptic curve multiplication takes a point P and a whole number k and produces kP, which is defined as P + P + P + ... + P with k terms. The book defines multiplication in the standard way that extends addition, and notes that k is sometimes confusingly called an exponent in this case (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In Bitcoin the point is the generator point and the multiplier is the private key, so the public key is K = k times G.\n\nThis single operation is the heart of Bitcoin's cryptography. It is easy in one direction and impossible, for any practical purpose, to reverse: the book calls it a trap door function, which is easy to do in one direction, multiplication, and impossible to do in the reverse direction, division (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Someone with a private key can easily create the public key and share it knowing that no one can reverse the function and calculate the private key from the public key; the same trick makes unforgeable digital signatures possible.\n\nThe book illustrates multiplication on the curve over the real numbers: adding a point to itself corresponds to drawing the tangent at the point, finding where it intersects the curve again, and reflecting that point in the x-axis, and its figure shows G, 2G and 4G obtained this way (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In real software the multiplication is performed by a library, such as libsecp256k1, which many Bitcoin implementations use for the elliptic curve math (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nAdding G to itself k times, where k is a 256-bit number, would be impossible. The double-and-add method avoids it by reading the bits of k. Starting from the leading bit, the method doubles the running point for every further bit and adds P whenever the bit is 1. For the book's example key, which has 253 bits of which 124 are set, this means 252 doublings and 123 additions, a few hundred operations instead of an astronomically large number; the chapter's checks compute these counts and verify that the toolkit's result for this key matches the public key printed in the book. They also check that (a + b) times G equals aG plus bG for sample multipliers, which is the algebraic law the method relies on.\n\nMultiplication is the fast direction only. The reverse calculation, finding k when K is known, is the discrete logarithm problem described in the next concept. Also do not confuse point multiplication with multiplying two points, which is not defined: the multiplier is always a plain whole number. Finally, the fact that a short loop of additions suffices for the forward direction does not make the chapter's toolkit safe for real use; its own description says so, and the concept on cryptographic libraries returns to the point." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#PointMultiplication> .
chx:S_DiscreteLogarithm a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Discrete logarithm and brute-force search"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Discrete logarithm and brute-force search" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 18 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Curve ;
    skos:definition "The discrete logarithm problem asks for the multiplier when the product is known. In elliptic curve terms: given the public key K and the generator point G, find the private key k with K = kG. The book calls this reverse operation 'finding the discrete logarithm' and says that it is as difficult as trying all possible values of k, that is, a brute-force search (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A brute-force search is simply the method of trying every candidate in turn until one fits.\n\nThe whole security of Bitcoin's ownership rule rests on the difficulty of this problem. If anyone could compute k from K, every public key that has ever appeared on the blockchain would reveal its private key, and anyone could spend anyone's coins. This is why the book calls elliptic curve multiplication a one-way function and bases the entire scheme on it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe same pattern of an easy forward step and a hard reverse step occurs in the exponentiation of prime numbers that the book names as another suitable function (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), and it can be seen in a small group. In the numbers modulo 101, raising 2 to the power 9 gives 7. Going backwards, from 7 to the exponent 9, requires trying exponents one after another until one fits, and the chapter's check does exactly that and stops at 9. Every step of the forward direction is a single call of pow, while the reverse direction needs a search.\n\nFor a private key of 256 bits the number of candidates is about 10 to the 77, far more than any computer could try. The standard's table of properties gives secp256k1 a security strength of 128 bits for a 256-bit field, which it describes as the approximate number of bits of security the parameters offer (Certicom Research, 2010). That figure is half of the 256-bit field size, so the book's statement that the problem is as difficult as brute force over all values of k is best read as a simplification: a search over every key would correspond to 256 bits of security and not 128. The practical conclusion stands either way, because 2 to the 128 operations remain out of reach.\n\nThe difficulty of the discrete logarithm is an assumption about the present state of mathematics and computing, not a proved theorem. The book is careful to speak of what is infeasible 'using the computers and algorithms available today' (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Reasoning from this assumption, a good habit is to make no stronger claim than the source does, and to keep in mind that the strength of 128 bits holds only if the private key is chosen at random from the full range." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#DiscreteLogarithm> .
chx:S_CryptoLibrary a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Cryptographic library (libsecp256k1)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Cryptographic library (libsecp256k1)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 19 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Curve ;
    skos:definition "A cryptographic library is a collection of ready-made routines for cryptographic calculations that programs call instead of writing the mathematics themselves. For the elliptic curve math of Bitcoin the book names the libsecp256k1 cryptographic library and observes that many Bitcoin implementations use it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The library carries out the point arithmetic, the multiplication and the signature operations of the curve that this level has just described.\n\nCryptographic code must be exactly right, because an error that passes every ordinary test can still leak a key or accept a forged signature. The book's advice about random numbers shows the attitude: do not write your own code, study the documentation of the library you choose, and remember that correct implementation is critical to the security of the keys (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The authors of the Schnorr signature standard make the same point about verification: to be safe for use in a consensus system, the verification algorithm must be completely specified at the byte level, so that nobody can construct a signature that is valid to some verifiers but not all (Wuille et al., 2020).\n\nThe reference implementation of Chapter 3, Bitcoin Core, and the wrapper libraries that call it are the kind of shared software that Bitcoin users rely on; a cryptographic library is the same idea one level lower, below the wallet and the node. When a wallet developer in another language implements a wallet, the library is what ensures that every implementation derives the same public key from the same private key.\n\nThe toolkit that accompanies this chapter shows what such a library does. The point addition and multiplication of the curve take thirteen lines of Python, fewer than twenty, and with them the toolkit reproduces the public key of the book's example, the addresses of the later sections and the bech32 examples. Its results were compared with Bitcoin Core 31.1, which gave the same addresses for the same keys, and with the reference code that the book uses for bech32. A library does the same arithmetic with the care that production use demands.\n\nThe toolkit is written for reading. Its own description says that it is not for use with real funds, because it is not constant-time, which means that the time it takes depends on the key, and because it takes no care over where randomness comes from. Never copy educational code into a wallet. The comparison with independent software also shows how trust is built in this field: not by believing one implementation, but by checking that several independent ones agree." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#CryptoLibrary> .
chx:S_Hashing a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Hash functions"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Hash functions" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 20 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Keys ;
    skos:definition "A hash function is the second tool, next to the elliptic curve, from which Bitcoin addresses are built. This level introduces the general idea and the three functions the chapter uses: SHA256, RIPEMD-160 and the combination of the two that Bitcoin calls HASH160. Together they allow a long public key, or a script of thousands of bytes, to be replaced by a short and reliable fingerprint that can be copied, printed and checked.\n\nThe book states the practical problem in these terms: Bitcoin already contains several data structures much larger than 65 bytes that need to be securely referenced in other parts of Bitcoin using the smallest amount of data that was secure (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A hash function solves the problem of referring to a large object by a small one without losing the guarantee that the reference identifies exactly that object.\n\nA hash function works by consuming its input in fixed-size blocks and stirring each block into a small block of internal state, so that the state at the end depends on every bit of the input and on the order in which the bits arrived. Three properties are what make the result usable as a reference. The output has the same length whatever the input is, so a reference to a short key and a reference to a long script cost the same. Changing any part of the input changes about half the bits of the output, so two inputs that differ slightly have outputs with nothing visibly in common. And the function cannot be run backwards, so a reference can be published without publishing what it refers to - which is exactly what an address does. Chaining two different functions, as Bitcoin does when it applies the second to the output of the first, shortens the result and means that a weakness found in one of them is not on its own a weakness in the pair (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (National Institute of Standards and Technology, 2015).\n\nThe reader meets these functions at every step of the chapter and then everywhere else in the course. Here they turn a public key into the twenty bytes an address commits to, and they produce the four checksum bytes that protect the text of the address. In chapter 2 the same arithmetic gave the identifier of a transaction and the root of a block's Merkle tree; in chapter 3 it gave the identifier of a block; in chapter 5 it is the engine of the key-stretching step that turns a recovery code into a seed. The functions themselves are published standards rather than Bitcoin's own, which is why this course quotes a national standard for the first of them (National Institute of Standards and Technology, 2015).\n\nThe caution is about strength and about length. A hash is only as strong as the shorter of the function and the output: cutting a thirty-two byte result down to twenty bytes, as Bitcoin does, lowers the work an attacker needs, and the three attacks described later in this chapter are the reason the newer addresses commit to thirty-two bytes instead. And one of the two functions used here is no longer available in every current software library, which is why this course states the lengths it produces and cites them to their standard rather than recomputing them in the browser (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Hashing> .
chx:S_HashFunction a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Hash function"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Hash function" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 21 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Hashing ;
    skos:definition "A hash function is a function that takes a potentially large amount of data, scrambles it (hashes it), and outputs a fixed amount of data. A cryptographic hash function will always produce the same output when given the same input, and a secure function also makes it impractical for somebody to choose a different input that produces a previously seen output (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The standard that defines the SHA family describes its algorithms as ones that generate digests of messages, which are used to detect whether messages have been changed since the digests were generated (National Institute of Standards and Technology, 2015).\n\nThese two properties make the output a commitment to the input, which is a promise that, in practice, only input x will produce output X (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book illustrates this with a question whose answer is revealed later: it publishes the hash of its answer now, and after the guess has been made it shows that the answer, put into the hash function, gives exactly the published value. In Bitcoin the same trick lets a receiver publish a small commitment to a public key or a script and reveal the full object only when it is used.\n\nHash functions occur throughout this book. Chapter 1's proof of work is a search for an input whose hash has a required form, Chapter 3 verified that a downloaded release file has the published hash, and this chapter uses hashes for addresses and for checksums. The program command sha256sum, which the book uses in its example, computes one of them from the command line.\n\nThe chapter's checks show the behaviour on the book's own example. The sentence the book hashes, followed by the line break that the echo command adds, has the SHA256 value 94d7a772612c8f2f2ec609d41f5bd3d04a5aa1dfe3582f04af517d396a302e4e, as printed in the book, and the same input gives it every time. Two inputs that differ in a single bit, the letter a and the character just before it in the ASCII table, give outputs that differ in 139 of their 256 bits, a little more than the half that one would expect from an unrelated pair: a tiny change in the input scrambles the output completely, so a near-miss reveals nothing about the right input.\n\nA hash is not an encryption: there is no key and no way to recover the input from the output except by guessing it. For the same reason it is not a good way to hide a small set of possible inputs. If the answer can only be one of a handful of years, anyone can hash all the candidates and compare, so a commitment hides a secret only when the set of possible secrets is huge. Finally, 'secure' is relative to output size: the shorter the output, the easier it is to find collisions, as the discussion of collision attacks later in the chapter shows." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#HashFunction> .
chx:S_Sha256 a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "SHA256"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "SHA256" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 22 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Hashing ;
    skos:definition "SHA256 is the member of the SHA family that produces 256 bits (32 bytes) of output, and the book calls it the function most commonly used in Bitcoin and considers it to be very secure (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Its output is less than half the size of the original Bitcoin public keys, which were 65 bytes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); the chapter's checks confirm that 32 is smaller than half of 65.\n\nBitcoin leans on SHA256 for many jobs. This chapter meets it as the first step of HASH160, as the double hash that gives base58check its checksum and as the function the book recommends for turning random bits into a private key candidate. Using one well-studied function in all these places keeps the system simple, and it means that a weakness in it would affect everything at once.\n\nThe command-line example in the book uses the tool sha256sum. Python offers the same function in its hashlib module, and the chapter's checks use it throughout. The hashlib module also provides functions of other families, but the Python that runs inside this course's browser pages has no RIPEMD-160, which is why some examples in this chapter are given as data.\n\nThe input can have any length and the output always has 32 bytes. The three-byte message abc has the SHA256 digest that begins ba7816bf, and the empty message and a message of a million bytes also give 32 bytes. The chapter's checks print the length for several inputs. Applying the function twice, as base58check does, is simply passing the 32-byte output as the input of a second call.\n\nA 256-bit output does not mean 256 bits of security for every attack. For finding two inputs with the same hash the work is about the square root of the number of outputs, so the best case is 128 bits, and for the 160-bit RIPEMD-160 it is 80 bits; the book's discussion of collision attacks makes exactly this point (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Choose the right function and the right question: preimage resistance, second preimage resistance and collision resistance are three different properties." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Sha256> .
chx:S_Ripemd160 a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "RIPEMD-160"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "RIPEMD-160" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 23 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Hashing ;
    skos:definition "RIPEMD-160 is a hash function whose output is 160 bits (20 bytes). The book describes it as one of the other, slightly less secure hash functions that produce smaller output than SHA256 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Its place in Bitcoin is as the second step of the commitment to a public key, applied to the SHA256 digest of the key.\n\nA smaller output means a shorter address and a smaller transaction. The original version of Bitcoin made commitments to public keys by first hashing the key with SHA256 and then hashing that output with RIPEMD-160, which produced a 20-byte commitment, and the book adds that Satoshi Nakamoto never stated the reasons for this choice (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The honest statement is therefore that the design is known and the motive is not.\n\nRIPEMD-160 is met in Bitcoin in three kinds of place: the 20-byte commitments inside legacy addresses, the 20-byte witness programs of version 0 pay to witness public key hash outputs, and the commitments to scripts in pay to script hash outputs, all of which later sections describe. The checksums of the next branch use SHA256 alone, applied twice.\n\nIn Python the call hashlib.new('ripemd160', data) computes it where the underlying library supports the function, and the chapter's checks use it to reproduce the book's addresses. The course's browser pages do not provide it in their Python, so examples that depend on it are given as data there. Because it works on its input in the same way for every call, two parties that apply SHA256 and then RIPEMD-160 to the same public key obtain the same 20 bytes.\n\nThe size of the output limits security. The book states that for a secure 160-bit algorithm the chance of independently finding an input for an existing commitment is 1 in 2 to the 160, a preimage attack, but that when an attacker can influence the original input, the strength drops to the square root, 1 in 2 to the 80 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For this reason newer address types use SHA256 alone, which gives commitments of 32 bytes, as the later section on segwit outputs explains." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Ripemd160> .
chx:S_Hash160 a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "HASH160"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "HASH160" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 24 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Hashing ;
    skos:definition "HASH160 is the name of the combination RIPEMD160(SHA256(K)), in which a public key K, or any other data, is first passed into SHA256 and the resulting digest is then passed into RIPEMD-160. The book writes the formula A = RIPEMD160(SHA256(K)), where K is the public key and A is the resulting commitment, a 160-bit (20-byte) number (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The name HASH160 is also that of the script operation OP_HASH160, which performs the same calculation inside a script.\n\nThe combination gives Bitcoin a short, fixed-size reference to an object of any length. The same construction is used for both main legacy outputs: the commitment to a public key in a pay to public key hash output and the commitment to a script in a pay to script hash output, where the book says that it uses the same HASH160 mechanism, RIPEMD160(SHA256(script)) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nFor the book's example private key the chapter's checks compute HASH160 of the compressed public key and obtain bbc1e42a39d05a4cc61752d6963b7f69d09bb27b, whose base58check form with version byte 0 is the address 1J7mdg5rbQyUHENYdx39WVWK7fsLpEoXZy. Hashing the uncompressed form of the same public key gives the different value 211b74ca4686f81efda5641767fc84ef16dafe0b and the different address 1424C2F4bC9JidNjjTUZCbUxv6Sa1Mt62x. The values match the output scripts that Bitcoin Core 31.1 reported when asked to validate the two addresses (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe calculation is mechanical. Take the bytes of the public key, compute SHA256, compute RIPEMD-160 of that digest, and keep the 20 bytes that result. In an output script the operation OP_HASH160 replaces the top item of the stack by its HASH160, so that the comparison with a stored commitment can be made by the script itself, as the sections on legacy scripts show (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nHASH160 commits to bytes, not to a mathematical point, so the same key written in two formats gives two different commitments. The book stresses that a single private key can produce a public key expressed in two formats, compressed and uncompressed, that produce two different addresses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The strength is that of a 160-bit function, no more, and the book recommends newer address types for this reason." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Hash160> .
chx:S_Format a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Format"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Format" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 25 ;
    skos:definition "This branch is about writing the same information down in different ways. A private key, a public key and a hash commitment are all numbers, and a number can be shown in binary, in hexadecimal, in a compact alphabet with a built-in checksum, or in a two-dimensional barcode. The choice of representation never changes the number, but it decides how long the text is, how easily a person can read it aloud, how likely a copying mistake is and whether the software can notice the mistake.\n\nRepresentation matters in Bitcoin because mistakes are expensive. The book points out that any mistake made in copying a commitment would result in the bitcoins being sent to an unspendable output, causing them to be lost forever (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A payment cannot be recalled by a bank, so the text that Alice copies from Bob must either be correct or be recognised as incorrect before the money moves. All the formats of this branch are answers to that single concern, and the checksums in particular turn silent errors into visible ones.\n\nThe formats appear in every wallet. Hexadecimal and raw binary are used internally in software and rarely shown to users, while the wallet import format is used for importing and exporting keys between wallets and for printing them as barcodes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Addresses use the base58check or the bech32 family of encodings, and the first character or the first few characters of such a string tell a trained eye which kind of data it is.\n\nThe branch has three levels of content. The first covers the basic ways to count and store data, bits, bytes, number bases and barcodes. The second covers the encodings of keys: the compressed and the uncompressed public key, the wallet import format of a private key, and the oddly named compressed private key. The third covers checksummed text: the checksum, the base58check procedure built from it, the version prefix that identifies the data type and the choice between the main network and the test network.\n\nNever judge two strings to be different data merely because they look different, and never judge them equal merely because they begin alike. The book's central example is a single private key that appears in three different formats and a single public key that appears in two formats and yields two different addresses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Keeping the data and its representation apart in the mind is the central discipline of this branch." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Format> .
chx:S_Representation a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Representation of data"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Representation of data" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 26 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Format ;
    skos:definition "The representation of data is the way a value is written down: as a count of bits, as a sequence of bytes, as digits in some number base, or as a picture that a camera can read. One and the same number has all of these forms at once, and none of them is more real than the others. This level gathers the three basic notions on which the encodings of the rest of the chapter build, so that a later section can speak of 65 bytes, of 130 hexadecimal digits or of a scanned code without having to explain itself again.\n\nEvery length that the chapter quotes, 32 bytes, 65 bytes, 130 hexadecimal digits, is a statement about a representation, and every conversion between formats is a change of representation. A reader who is not at ease with bits, bytes and number bases will find the later sections impossible to follow, so they are defined here before they are used.\n\nA representation works by fixing two things: an alphabet of symbols, and a rule for reading a sequence of them as a number. The number is what exists; the text is one of many ways of writing it. Choosing a larger alphabet makes the text shorter, because each symbol carries more of the number - a byte written in base sixteen takes two characters, in base fifty-eight roughly one and a third, in base thirty-two exactly one and three fifths - and choosing which symbols the alphabet leaves out decides how safely a person can copy the text by hand. That is why the formats of this chapter differ: one drops the characters that look alike, another uses one case only so the text can be spoken and packed into a barcode. A picture such as a barcode is the same choice taken further, with the alphabet made of marks rather than letters (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nThe reader meets these three notions in every length the chapter quotes. A private key is thirty-two bytes, which is sixty-four hexadecimal digits and fifty-one characters in the wallet's export format; an uncompressed public key is sixty-five bytes and a hundred and thirty hexadecimal digits; a native-segwit address of this chapter is forty-two characters of a thirty-two symbol alphabet. Each of those statements is about a representation and not about a different value, and the chapter's own figures for them are computed rather than quoted (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe caution is that a conversion can lose information even when it looks harmless. Case is the common trap: the older format distinguishes upper from lower case and the newer one does not, so a reader who writes an older address in capitals has destroyed it, while writing a newer one in capitals is permitted and changes nothing. The second trap is the leading zero: a number has no leading zeros but a byte string does, and an encoding that forgets them produces text of the wrong length, which is why the procedure later in this chapter handles them as a separate step (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Representation> .
chx:S_BitsAndBytes a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bits and bytes"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bits and bytes" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 27 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Representation ;
    skos:definition "A bit is a binary digit, which has the value 0 or 1, and a byte is a group of 8 bits. Sizes in Bitcoin are quoted in both units, and the chapter moves between them freely: the book describes a private key as 256 bits shown as 64 hexadecimal digits of 4 bits each (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), a hash commitment as 160 bits or 20 bytes, and a public key as 65 bytes or 33 bytes.\n\nThe units decide how much space data takes and how large the space of possible values is. A value of 256 bits can take 2 to the 256 different values, which is the reason for the book's statement that the private key space is unfathomably large (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), while a value of 20 bytes has 160 bits and therefore only 2 to the 160 possible values. When the chapter later compares the strength of different commitments, it is comparing numbers of bits.\n\nEverywhere in the chapter you will find the conversion between the two units. The book gives the uncompressed public key as a 520-bit number of 130 hexadecimal digits, and the compressed one as a number of 264 bits in 66 hexadecimal digits (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). These are the same lengths as 65 and 33 bytes, because 65 times 8 is 520 and 33 times 8 is 264, and because a hexadecimal digit carries 4 bits, so two digits make one byte; the chapter's checks verify these equalities.\n\nIn Python a sequence of bytes is the type bytes, bytes.fromhex turns a string of hexadecimal digits into bytes, and the method hex() turns bytes back into digits. The built-in function len returns the number of bytes, and an integer has a bit_length() method. These few tools are all that the chapter's examples need to move between a key as a number, as bytes and as text.\n\nThe most frequent mistake is to mix up the number of digits with the number of bytes: 64 hexadecimal digits are 32 bytes, not 64. Another is to forget that a byte is an unsigned quantity, so that the byte 0x80 stands for 128 and not for a negative number. Count carefully and state the unit." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#BitsAndBytes> .
chx:S_NumberBase a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Number base (hexadecimal, base58, base64)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Number base (hexadecimal, base58, base64)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 28 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Representation ;
    skos:definition "A number base, also called a radix, is the number of distinct symbols that a notation uses for writing numbers. The traditional decimal system uses 10 numerals, 0 through 9, and the hexadecimal system uses 16, with the letters A through F as the six additional symbols. The book adds that a number written in hexadecimal is shorter than the equivalent decimal representation, and that base64 representation uses 26 lowercase letters, 26 capital letters, 10 numerals and 2 more characters such as + and /, in order to transmit binary data over text-based media such as email (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe bigger the alphabet, the fewer symbols are needed for the same number, which is why compact formats are attractive for things that people have to copy. The book introduces base58 for this reason: it is a similar encoding to base64 that uses upper- and lowercase letters and numbers, but omits some characters that are frequently mistaken for one another and can appear identical when displayed in certain fonts (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A larger alphabet gains length, a careful alphabet gains reliability, and base58 is a compromise.\n\nHexadecimal is the notation in which the chapter prints keys, hashes and coordinates, and base58 is the notation of legacy addresses and of keys in the wallet import format. Base64 is mentioned by the book only for comparison. Bech32, which later sections introduce, is a base 32 notation. The chapter's checks confirm that the base58 alphabet has 58 distinct characters, that it is the base64 alphabet without the six characters 0, O, l, I, + and /, and that the same private key needs fewer symbols in each larger base.\n\nWriting a number in base b means finding its digits when it is divided repeatedly by b. In Python int(text, 16) reads hexadecimal and int(text, 2) reads binary, so that ff and 11111111 both give 255. For base58 the book gives the alphabet 123456789ABCDEFGHJKLMNPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijkmnopqrstuvwxyz, whose symbols stand for the values 0 to 57 in order (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); a number is divided by 58 again and again, and each remainder selects one symbol. The one-expression example of the wallet import format in a later concept does exactly that.\n\nA symbol means different things in different bases, and an alphabet is meaningful only together with its order. The digit 1 is the value one in decimal and hexadecimal but is the first symbol, the value 0, in the base58 alphabet, a fact that explains why leading zero bytes appear as the character 1 in addresses. Base conversion also needs care with leading zeros, which a number does not have but a byte string does." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#NumberBase> .
chx:S_QrCode a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "QR code"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "QR code" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 29 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Representation ;
    skos:definition "A QR code is a two-dimensional barcode, a square pattern of dots, that a phone camera can read to obtain a short piece of text such as an address or an invoice. Wallets use it because it removes typing altogether: the book notes that QR codes are commonly used to share addresses and invoices between wallets (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Chapter 2 already showed a QR code that carried a payment request with an address, an amount and a label.\n\nThe size of the text decides the size of the picture. The book observes that a mixed-case alphabet requires extra space in QR codes, so that the codes need to be larger at the same resolution or become harder to scan quickly (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The proposal that introduced bech32 lists the same point among its motivations: base58 needs a lot of space in QR codes, as it cannot use the alphanumeric mode (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). A smaller picture scans faster and is more tolerant of poor cameras.\n\nQR codes are met next to cash registers, on invoices, on paper wallets and on screens. The proposal for pay to script hash named them as a reason for short commitments, saying that a 20-byte hash is short enough to scan from a QR code or easily copied and pasted (Andresen, 2012). The book's figure of the same bech32 address encoded twice shows the effect in practice: the lowercase version and the uppercase version give two QR codes of visibly different size and complexity (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe bech32 specification says that encoders must always output a lowercase string, and that an uppercase version may be produced outside the encoding process, for presentation or for QR code use; decoders must not accept strings that mix uppercase and lowercase letters (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). Inside QR codes uppercase should be used, because uppercase permits the alphanumeric mode, which the proposal describes as 45 percent more compact than the normal byte mode (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). The chapter's checks confirm that the uppercase form of the book's address decodes to the same witness program as the lowercase form, and that a mixed-case form is rejected.\n\nThe saving applies only to alphabets that fit the alphanumeric mode, which is why base58 text, with its mixed case, cannot profit from it. Another caution is that the picture is only the address in a different form: whatever the camera reads is still text, and a careful wallet shows the decoded text so that the person can compare it with what was intended." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#QrCode> .
chx:S_KeyEncoding a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Key encoding"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Key encoding" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 30 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Format ;
    skos:definition "A key encoding is a particular way of writing a key down as bytes or as text. The same private key has several (hexadecimal, wallet import format, wallet import format with a compression flag), and the same public key has two (compressed and uncompressed). All of them stand for the same underlying number or point, and the book stresses that any one format can easily be converted to any other (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nSoftware that exports or imports keys must agree on the encoding, otherwise the receiving wallet may look for the wrong addresses. The book explains the case concretely: a wallet that imports keys from an older wallet needs to know whether to scan the blockchain for 65-byte keys and the commitments to them, or for 33-byte keys and their commitments, and failure to scan for the right type can lead to the user not being able to spend the full balance (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe encodings work by wrapping the same value in a few bytes that say how to read it. A public key is a point with two coordinates; writing both gives sixty-five bytes, but the curve's equation determines the second coordinate up to its sign, so a single byte recording which sign applies is enough and the compressed form is thirty-three bytes. The export form of a private key is the thirty-two secret bytes with a version byte in front, a checksum behind and, when the key belongs to a compressed public key, one extra byte in the middle whose only job is to tell the importing wallet which of the two public keys to derive. Nothing is encrypted and nothing is compressed in the ordinary sense: every one of these forms can be converted into any other, which is what the book means when it says the formats are interchangeable (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets the encodings whenever a key crosses a boundary between programs. A key handed to a learner in a tutorial is in hexadecimal; a key exported by a wallet for a backup is in the text format that begins with a recognisable character; a key inside a transaction is the compressed binary form. The place where the distinction bites is an import, because the receiving wallet has to decide which addresses to look for, and the chapter quotes the book's warning about exactly that case (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe caution is that one of these names is a misnomer and the book says so. Nothing about a private key is compressed; what the extra byte records is that the public key derived from it should be the compressed one. A learner who reads the name literally will expect the key itself to be shorter, find that it is not, and distrust the rest of the section. The second caution is that these text forms carry the secret in plain sight: an exported private key is the funds, and the chapter's section on passphrase protection exists because a backup of one is as sensitive as the wallet it came from (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#KeyEncoding> .
chx:S_CompressedPublicKey a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Compressed public key"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Compressed public key" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 31 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_KeyEncoding ;
    skos:definition "A compressed public key is an encoding of a public key that stores the x coordinate and one extra byte instead of both coordinates. When Bitcoin was first written its developers only knew how to create 65-byte public keys; a later developer found an alternative encoding that used only 33 bytes and was backward compatible with all Bitcoin full nodes at the time, so that the protocol did not need to change (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The 33-byte keys are called compressed public keys and the original 65-byte keys are called uncompressed public keys.\n\nSmaller keys make smaller transactions, and smaller transactions let more payments fit into the same block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Omitting the 256-bit y coordinate reduces the size of the key by almost 50 percent, which the chapter's checks put at 49.2 percent, and the saving is repeated in every transaction that reveals a key. Compressed public keys are now the default in almost all Bitcoin software and were required for certain features added by later protocol upgrades (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe compressed form of the book's example key is 03F028892BAD7ED57D2FB57BF33081D5CFCF6F9ED3D3D7F159C2E2FFF579DC341A, a number of 264 bits written in 66 hexadecimal digits (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is the form that modern wallets hash into addresses, and it is the form that the 'compressed' private key format of a later concept announces. Older wallets that still use the uncompressed form are the reason why software must be able to read both.\n\nThe method rests on the curve equation. Since the point (x, y) satisfies y squared = x cubed + 7, knowing x leaves only two possible values of y, which are a number and its negative modulo p, the mirror pair of the curve's symmetry (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). One of the two is even and the other is odd, because p is odd, so a single prefix byte settles the choice: 02 means that y is even and 03 means that y is odd (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For the book's key the y coordinate ends in B, an odd digit, hence the prefix 03. The chapter's checks recover the full point from the 33 bytes by computing a modular square root, and they find the original coordinates.\n\nCompression changes the bytes that are hashed. The same private key gives two public key formats, and if the compressed key is turned into a commitment with HASH160 it produces a different commitment from the uncompressed key, leading to a different address; the private key is identical for both addresses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A wallet that scans for only one of the two forms will miss funds that were paid to the other." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#CompressedPublicKey> .
chx:S_UncompressedPublicKey a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Uncompressed public key"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Uncompressed public key" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 32 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_KeyEncoding ;
    skos:definition "An uncompressed public key is the original encoding of a public key: the prefix byte 04 followed by the 32-byte x coordinate and the 32-byte y coordinate, 65 bytes in all. The book writes it as 04 x y and shows the example key as a 520-bit number of 130 hexadecimal digits (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The shortest version of Bitcoin public keys known to the early developers was this 65-byte form, the equivalent of 130 characters in hexadecimal (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nUnderstanding the uncompressed form is necessary because it has not disappeared. The book says that some software still needs to support uncompressed public keys, such as a wallet application importing private keys from an older wallet, and that such a wallet must scan for both kinds of key and for commitments to both (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The funds paid to an address built from an uncompressed key can only be spent by presenting that same uncompressed key.\n\nEarly Bitcoin addresses, some of which still hold funds, were built from uncompressed keys. The book's example key gives the P2PKH address 1424C2F4bC9JidNjjTUZCbUxv6Sa1Mt62x in this form, and Bitcoin Core 31.1 returned the same address when asked for the address of that uncompressed key and called it valid (Bitcoin Core, 2026). This is a different address from the one derived from the compressed form of the same key.\n\nThe prefix byte 04 tells the reader that both coordinates follow. To obtain the uncompressed form from a compressed one, software performs the square-root calculation described in the previous concept, and to compress, it keeps x and the parity of y. The two conversions lose nothing in either direction, which the chapter's checks demonstrate by a round trip on the example key.\n\nSince the compressed and uncompressed keys are two encodings of one point, they are easily taken for two different keys. They correspond to the same private key, yet they lead to different HASH160 values and different legacy addresses, and a user who exports a key from an old wallet without recording which form it used may later look in the wrong place for the balance." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#UncompressedPublicKey> .
chx:S_WalletImportFormat a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Wallet import format (WIF)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Wallet import format (WIF)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 33 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_KeyEncoding ;
    skos:definition "The wallet import format, abbreviated WIF, is the text form in which a private key is exported from one wallet and imported into another. It is the base58check encoding of the version byte 0x80 followed by the 32-byte private key, and, for keys that produce compressed public keys, an extra byte 01 at the end (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For the book's example key the WIF is 5J3mBbAH58CpQ3Y5RNJpUKPE62SQ5tfcvU2JpbnkeyhfsYB1Jcn, and the compressed WIF is KxFC1jmwwCoACiCAWZ3eXa96mBM6tb3TYzGmf6YwgdGWZgawvrtJ.\n\nA raw 64-digit hexadecimal number is awkward to copy and carries no protection against typing errors. The WIF gives the key a recognisable first character and a checksum, so that a wallet can reject a mistyped key instead of importing a different one. The book says that the WIF is used for import and export of keys between wallets and often for QR code representations of private keys (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe WIF is met when someone moves a single key from an old wallet to a new one, and in older instructions for paper wallets and key backups. The book notes, however, that modern wallets generate all their keys from a single seed and rarely allow a single key to be imported or exported, so that the information about private key formats is mainly of interest to anyone needing compatibility with early Bitcoin wallets (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A later concept on key export describes what Bitcoin Core did about the question.\n\nThe procedure has four steps. Put the version byte 0x80 in front of the key, add the byte 01 behind it if the key is for compressed public keys, append the first four bytes of the double SHA256 of the result, and write the whole in the base58 alphabet. The chapter's checks do this in one expression and obtain the book's WIF string, and with the extra byte they obtain the book's compressed string. Decoding reverses the steps and verifies the four checksum bytes. Without the suffix the result is 51 characters long and with it 52, and the first character is always 5 for the first and K or L for the second.\n\nA WIF string is a private key, to be treated like one: anyone who sees it can spend the funds. Do not paste it into web pages, do not photograph it and do not mix up the two forms. The first character is the safety signal: 5 means that the wallet must look for the uncompressed public key and its addresses, and K or L means that it must look for the compressed ones (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#WalletImportFormat> .
chx:S_CompressedPrivateKey a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Compressed private key"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Compressed private key" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 34 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_KeyEncoding ;
    skos:definition "The commonly used term 'compressed private key' is a misnomer. When a private key is exported as WIF-compressed it is actually one byte longer than an 'uncompressed' private key, because it has an added one-byte suffix, shown as 01 in hexadecimal, which signifies that the private key is from a newer wallet and should only be used to produce compressed public keys (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Private keys are not themselves compressed and cannot be compressed.\n\nThe distinction prevents a practical error. The term really means 'private key from which only compressed public keys should be derived', and the term 'uncompressed private key' really means 'private key from which only uncompressed public keys should be derived' (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book therefore advises referring to the export format as WIF-compressed or WIF and not to the private key itself as compressed, to avoid further confusion.\n\nThe flag shows up in exported keys. The hexadecimal form of the book's example with the suffix is the original 64 digits followed by 01, and the base58check form begins with K or L. The base58 version prefix is the same, 0x80, for both WIF and WIF-compressed; the extra byte changes the length of the number and therefore the first character from 5 to K or L (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book offers an analogy: the difference is like that between the decimal numbers 100 and 99, where 100 is one digit longer and also has a prefix of 1 instead of 9. As the length changes, it affects the prefix, and in base58 the prefix 5 changes to K or L as the length increases by one byte (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's checks confirm the mechanism for the extreme keys, the smallest and the largest possible 32-byte values: without the suffix the first character is 5, with it the first character is K or L, and the decoded payload has 34 bytes against 33 bytes without the suffix when the version byte is counted.\n\nThe formats are not used interchangeably. In a newer wallet that implements compressed public keys the private keys are only ever exported as WIF-compressed with a K or L prefix, and in an older implementation that does not use compressed public keys they are exported as WIF with a prefix of 5 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The goal is to signal to the importing wallet whether it must search the blockchain for compressed or uncompressed public keys and addresses. Treat the suffix as a message, not as a compression." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#CompressedPrivateKey> .
chx:S_Checksummed a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Checksummed text"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Checksummed text" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 35 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Format ;
    skos:definition "Checksummed text is text that carries a few extra characters computed from the rest, so that the reader of the text can detect a copying mistake. Base58check is the first such format in this chapter and bech32 is the second. This level gathers the notion of the checksum, the base58check procedure, the version prefix that is part of the data it protects, and the distinction between networks that the prefix expresses.\n\nAn address is typed, read aloud or copied by people, and a payment to a mistyped address is lost. A checksum is the cheapest defence: the wallet recomputes the check and refuses an address that does not match. The book states the benefit in a single sentence, that the check prevents a mistyped Bitcoin address from being accepted by the wallet software as a valid destination, an error that would otherwise result in loss of funds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA checksum works by adding characters that are a function of everything before them, so that the valid strings are a tiny scattered subset of all the strings that could be written. The older format of this chapter hashes the version byte together with the payload, twice, and appends the first four bytes of the result; a decoder recomputes the hash and compares. Because the four bytes are thirty-two bits, a string altered at random passes by chance about once in four thousand million attempts, and because a hash spreads any change over the whole output, there is no family of mistakes it is systematically blind to. What such a check cannot do is say where the mistake was or repair it - it only refuses - and the newer format of the next level was designed with that limitation in mind. The version byte sits inside the protected data rather than beside it, which is why altering the byte that decides the network also breaks the check (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nThe reader meets the check as the wallet's refusal. Pasting an address with one character changed produces an error rather than a payment, and that error is this section at work; the same check runs on an imported private key and on an extended key of chapter 5. The reader meets the version byte as the first character of the text, which is why an address beginning with one digit and an address beginning with another are recognisably different kinds, and why a key or an address for the test network cannot be mistaken for one on the main network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe caution is about what the guarantee covers. The check catches mistakes in copying; it cannot catch an address that was copied perfectly from the wrong place. A string substituted by malicious software, or taken from the wrong line of a message, is a valid address with a valid checksum and the payment is final. The second caution is that four bytes is a defence against accident and not against a determined search, which is the difference between a checksum and the cryptographic commitments elsewhere in this chapter (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Checksummed> .
chx:S_Checksum a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Checksum"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Checksum" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 36 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Checksummed ;
    skos:definition "A checksum is a short value derived from a piece of data and attached to it. Anyone who has the data and the attached value can recompute the check and see whether they still agree; if the data was altered, they will with overwhelming probability disagree. In base58check the checksum is an additional four bytes added to the end of the data being encoded, derived from the hash of that data, and therefore able to detect transcription and typing errors (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nDetection is worth a lot even without correction. When the decoding software calculates the checksum of the data and compares it with the one included in the code, a mismatch means that an error has been introduced and the data is invalid (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The wallet can then ask for the address again. The only alternative is to send the money and find out afterwards.\n\nChecksums are common outside Bitcoin, in the check digits of identification numbers and in file downloads. Inside the chapter they appear twice: as the four bytes of base58check, and as the six characters of a bech32 address, which the book describes as a checksum produced by a BCH code that is exactly 6 characters long (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In both cases the checksum carries no information of its own.\n\nThe check is only as strong as its length. Four bytes are 32 bits, so a string that was changed at random still passes the base58check test with probability 1 in 2 to the 32, about 2.3 times 10 to the minus 10, and six bech32 characters are 30 bits, giving less than 1 in 10 to the 9, which is the figure the bech32 proposal states for errors beyond the guaranteed ones (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). The chapter's checks recompute the four checksum bytes of the book's P2PKH address from its first 21 bytes and find them equal to the last four, and they show that changing one character of the address breaks the check.\n\nA checksum detects accident, not attack. Anyone who changes an address on purpose can compute a new valid checksum, so the check protects against typing mistakes and not against a malicious replacement of the whole string. It also says nothing about whether the address is the one you meant: a perfectly valid address that belongs to someone else passes every check." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Checksum> .
chx:S_Base58Check a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Base58check encoding"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Base58check encoding" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 37 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Checksummed ;
    skos:definition "Base58check is the encoding that Bitcoin's legacy addresses and exported private keys use. It combines three ingredients: a version byte in front of the data, a checksum of four bytes behind it, and the base58 alphabet, which consists of lowercase and capital letters and numbers without the four characters 0, O, l and I (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's alphabet is 123456789ABCDEFGHJKLMNPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijkmnopqrstuvwxyz, with 58 symbols.\n\nThe design serves people. Omitting the characters that look alike in certain fonts reduces misreading, the version prefix makes the type of data visible at a glance, and the checksum lets software refuse a damaged string. The book explains that this prevents a mistyped Bitcoin address from being accepted by the wallet software as a valid destination (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A shorter representation also takes less room on paper and in messages than a hexadecimal one.\n\nBase58check is used in the legacy P2PKH and P2SH addresses and in the wallet import format of private keys, and, as the book says, other data besides public key commitments are presented in it to make that data compact, easy to read and easy to check (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's table of version prefixes lists addresses, private keys and the extended public key among the data types that use it.\n\nTo encode, first add the version byte to the data. Then compute the checksum as the SHA256 of the SHA256 of the prefix followed by the data, the book's double-SHA checksum, and keep the first four bytes of the resulting 32-byte hash. Append the four bytes, and write the three-part result as a number in base58 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's checks do this for the book's key: the double SHA256 of the version byte and the key begins with the four bytes that the WIF string ends with when decoded, and the toolkit's decoder verifies them again. A leading version byte of zero becomes the character 1 in front of the text.\n\nBase58check is not secure encoding, and it is not even particularly efficient to compute. The bech32 proposal lists its limitations as a double SHA256 checksum that is slow and has no error-detection guarantees, a mixed case that is inconvenient to read aloud and to place in a QR code, and a decoding procedure that is complicated and relatively slow, and these are reasons why newer address types moved to another format (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). The book names the same weaknesses, adding that the format can detect errors but cannot help correct them (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Base58Check> .
chx:S_VersionPrefix a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Version prefix"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Version prefix" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 38 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Checksummed ;
    skos:definition "The version prefix, which the book calls the version byte, is the first byte of the data that base58check encodes, and it identifies the type of data. The prefix zero, 0x00 in hexadecimal, indicates that the data is the commitment of a legacy pay to public key hash output script; the book's table lists 0x00 for a P2PKH address, 0x05 for a P2SH address, 0x6F and 0xC4 for the testnet counterparts, 0x80 for a private key in WIF and 0x0488B21E for a BIP32 extended public key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe prefix makes data self-describing. Because the version byte is encoded together with the rest, each prefix gives encoded strings that begin with specific characters, which makes it easy for humans to identify the type of data and how to use it. This is what differentiates, for example, a base58check-encoded address that starts with 1 from a private key in WIF that starts with 5 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe resulting leading characters are the ones people recognise: 1 for P2PKH addresses, 3 for P2SH addresses, m or n for testnet P2PKH addresses, 2 for testnet P2SH addresses, 5, K or L for private keys in WIF and the letters xpub for extended public keys (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The extended public key belongs to the wallet structures that Chapter 5 will present. The chapter's checks encode data of each prefix and show the first characters that come out.\n\nThe leading character is a consequence of the numbers and not an extra field: the prefix byte is the most significant part of a number that is then written in base 58, so its value selects the first digit or the first few. For a 20-byte payload the version 0x00 gives the character 1, the version 0x05 gives 3, and the version 0xC4 gives 2; 0x6F gives m or n depending on the payload, as the checks show for the smallest and the largest payloads. For a 32-byte payload the version 0x80 gives 5, and with the suffix 01 it gives K or L.\n\nThe prefix identifies the type; it does not authenticate the data. A string that starts with 1 and passes the checksum test is a plausible P2PKH address, nothing more: it may belong to anyone, and it may belong to a key that nobody holds any longer. Nor does the prefix tell the payer which script the receiver will use to spend, since the version byte names a template and the template is the same for every receiver. The prefixes of the book's table apply to base58check only; bech32 addresses express the same two ideas, the network and the kind of output, through the human-readable part and the witness version, as a later section explains." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#VersionPrefix> .
chx:S_NetworkSelector a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Mainnet and testnet"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Mainnet and testnet" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 39 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Checksummed ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin software can work with more than one network. The main network is usually called mainnet, and a test network, testnet, is a separate chain whose name says what it is for. Bitcoin Core states that all content of its data directory, except the configuration file, is chain-specific, and it keeps the data of the main chain, testnet3, testnet4, signet and regtest in separate directories (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nAddresses must tell the networks apart, otherwise a payment prepared for the test network could be sent to the main network or the other way round. The version byte does the work for base58check: a P2PKH address uses 0x00 on the main network and 0x6F on the test network, and a P2SH address uses 0x05 and 0xC4, giving first characters 1, m or n, 3 and 2 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In bech32 addresses the human-readable part has the same function.\n\nThe book's table of human-readable parts for Bitcoin lists two entries: bc for Bitcoin mainnet and tb for Bitcoin testnet (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The proposal for bech32 shows both in its examples, for instance a mainnet pay to witness public key hash address that begins with bc1q and a testnet address for the same key that begins with tb1q (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). The chapter's checks read the first two characters from the book's mainnet address and from the proposal's testnet address.\n\nFor base58check the network is part of the data that the checksum protects, so changing the prefix byte of an address changes the text and breaks the check. For bech32 the human-readable part is mixed into the checksum computation: the checksum covers the human-readable part as well as the data part, which is why an address cannot be moved to another network by replacing the letters bc with tb (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). The consequence is that every wallet validates the network and the checksum in one step.\n\nTest coins are for testing. Do not read the validity of a testnet address as a statement about money, and do not send mainnet funds to a testnet address; the formats differ exactly so that software can stop you. Bitcoin Core lists several test chains, namely testnet3, testnet4, signet and regtest (Bitcoin Core, 2026), so a tutorial that speaks of 'the testnet' without naming one is ambiguous." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#NetworkSelector> .
chx:S_Address a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Address"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Address" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 40 ;
    skos:definition "This branch turns the keys of the first branch into something one person can hand to another. An address is a short string that a receiver gives to a payer so that the payer's wallet can build a transaction output that only the receiver can spend. In the book's story Alice wants to pay Bob, and the method she uses has to make sure that only Bob can later spend what he receives (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The branch follows the history of the answer, from the first and simplest scheme of the original Bitcoin paper, in which Alice pays a public key directly, to the addresses that wallets display today.\n\nThe public key itself is a poor thing to hand over. The book points out how long it is: imagine Bob reading the two 64-digit coordinates of his key to Alice over the telephone (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Even the shortest form known to the first developers took 65 bytes, equivalent to 130 characters in hexadecimal, and any mistake made in copying a value of that kind would send the bitcoins to an unspendable output and lose them for ever (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Every step in the history of addresses is an attempt to give the receiver a shorter, safer and more flexible thing to communicate.\n\nAddresses are what a user sees in a wallet. Chapter 1 introduced the Bitcoin address as the string by which a payment is directed, and Chapter 2 showed that a transaction output is the place where funds wait for their next spender. An address is a convenient way of writing down what an output script contains, and the same wallet can show several kinds of address, which differ in the script they stand for, in the alphabet in which they are written and in the checks they carry.\n\nThe branch has three parts. The first explains output scripts, input scripts and the stack machine that evaluates them, because every address kind is a name for one script template. The second covers the legacy kinds, the pay to public key output, the public key hash commitment of the P2PKH address and the script hash commitment of the P2SH address, together with the attacks that limit how short a hash can safely be. The third covers the segregated witness addresses, whose design answers the problems that the legacy kinds had (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nAn address is not an account and it is not a key. It contains no private information, it can be recomputed from a public key or a script, and it says nothing about who owns the funds. It is also not a promise: a valid address of the right kind is accepted by the wallet even if its owner has lost the key, so that the funds sent to it can never be spent. The remaining sections show how the checks built into each address kind reduce, but never remove, the chance of paying a wrong string." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Address> .
chx:S_Scripts a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Locking and unlocking scripts"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Locking and unlocking scripts" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 41 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Address ;
    skos:definition "A script, in Bitcoin, is a short program that decides whether a spend of an output is allowed. Every output carries one, called its output script, and every input that spends it carries another, called its input script. The two are run together by every full node that checks the transaction. This level of the chapter introduces both scripts and the small machine that runs them, because each address kind of the following levels is only a name for a particular output script.\n\nThe scripts are what turn the simple scheme of the original paper, a public key and a signature, into something that can express other conditions. The book explains that the first version of Bitcoin did not send payments directly to public keys but to a field called the output script, and authorised spends with a field called the input script, so that additional operations could be performed in addition to, or instead of, verifying that a signature corresponds to a public key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). An output script can, for example, contain two public keys and require two corresponding signatures in the spending input script.\n\nThe two scripts work as a single program assembled at spending time and run on a stack machine. A node takes the input script written by the spender, runs it, and the values it pushes are left on the stack; it then runs the output script that the earlier transaction attached to the funds, which consumes those values and tests them. For the commonest case the input script pushes a signature and a public key, and the output script checks that the public key hashes to the commitment recorded in it and that the signature matches both the key and the transaction being authorised. The spend is allowed when the machine finishes with a true value on top and nothing has failed. Two consequences explain the whole of the rest of this chapter: the condition is chosen by the receiver and recorded in the output, so an address is nothing but a compact way of telling a payer which output script to write; and because the condition is a program rather than a key, it can require two signatures, or a delay, or anything else the language can express (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets the scripts without seeing them. Every payment in chapter 2 was authorised this way, and every full node of chapter 3 ran the machine on it; a block explorer shows the two scripts side by side when a transaction is opened. In this chapter the scripts are met as the thing each address kind names: the sections that follow differ from one another only in which output script the address stands for, which is why they can be read quickly once this level is understood (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe caution is that this level is deliberately shallow. It gives only what is needed to read the address kinds, and the book promises the full treatment later; a learner who tries to settle questions about the language's operations or its limits from this level will not find the answers here. The second caution is that a script guards the funds and a mistake in one is not recoverable: an output script that nobody can satisfy locks the money for ever, which is why addresses, standard templates and published test vectors exist rather than hand-written scripts (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Scripts> .
chx:S_OutputScript a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Output script"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Output script" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 42 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Scripts ;
    skos:definition "An output script is the field of a transaction output that states the conditions under which the funds of that output may be spent. The book describes the arrangement as follows: bitcoins are received to an output script that acts like a public key, and bitcoin spending is authorised by an input script that acts like a signature (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The output script is therefore the lock, and the input script the key that opens it. In the specification of pay to script hash the same field is called by its internal name, the scriptPubKey (Andresen, 2012).\n\nBecause the lock is a program and not a fixed public key, the receiver can choose what must be shown to spend. The simplest lock asks for a signature that matches one public key. A richer lock can require a public key whose hash matches a stored value, several signatures, or a script that is revealed only when the funds are spent. All the address kinds of this chapter exist so that the receiver can choose among these locks and the payer can build the right one without understanding it.\n\nOutput scripts are met whenever a wallet or a block explorer displays an output. Bitcoin Core reports the script of an address when it is asked to validate it, and for the legacy address 1J7mdg5rbQyUHENYdx39WVWK7fsLpEoXZy it gives the hexadecimal string 76a914bbc1e42a39d05a4cc61752d6963b7f69d09bb27b88ac (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Reading it byte by byte, 76 stands for the operation that duplicates the top item, a9 for the operation that hashes it, 14 says that twenty bytes of data follow, the twenty bytes are the commitment to the public key, and 88 and ac stand for the verification of equality and the check of the signature (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe payer's wallet builds the output script from the receiver's address. It decodes the address, finds the kind and the data inside it, and writes the matching template with the data in place; that is the whole of the work a payer has to do. The chapter's checks rebuild the script above from the hash of the example key and find the same 25 bytes that Bitcoin Core reported. The opcode values used here are read from the source code of Bitcoin Core, which defines them as numbers, for instance OP_DUP as 0x76 and OP_CHECKSIG as 0xac (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nAn output script is data, not a promise of safety. A payer who builds a script that does not follow one of the accepted templates may create an output that nobody can spend, or one that anyone can spend. The book gives a specific warning for pay to script hash, saying that the output script must be exactly the template with no extra data or conditions, otherwise the bitcoins may be unspendable or spendable by anyone (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For the same reason payers should always use the address of the receiver and never assemble scripts by hand." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#OutputScript> .
chx:S_InputScript a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Input script"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Input script" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 43 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Scripts ;
    skos:definition "An input script is the field of a transaction input that provides what the output script being spent demands. Where the output script says what must be shown, the input script shows it. In the earliest scheme the input script consisted entirely of the spender's signature, and for a pay to public key hash output it consists of a signature followed by the public key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the language of the specification of pay to script hash, the input script is the scriptSig (Andresen, 2012).\n\nThe separation of the two scripts is what allows the receiver to keep information private until the moment of spending. An output that commits to a hash of a key does not reveal the key; the key appears for the first time in the input script of the spend, where a node checks it against the commitment. The same separation lets a payer create an output without knowing the details of the data that will later be needed to spend it.\n\nInput scripts are met in every spending transaction. The three examples of the chapter show the pattern: the input script of a pay to public key output is the signature alone, that of a pay to public key hash output is the signature and the public key, and that of a pay to script hash output is the signatures followed by the whole redeem script (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The specification of pay to script hash adds a restriction: validation fails if there are any operations other than pushing data in the input script (Andresen, 2012).\n\nWhen a node validates a spend, it takes the input script and the output script and runs them as one program. The book explains that you can combine them together, input script first, and then note that each piece of data, shown in angle brackets, is placed at the top of a list of items called a stack (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The data items of the input script are therefore pushed first, and the operations of the output script act on them afterwards. In the pay to public key hash example the signature is pushed first and the public key is placed on top of it.\n\nThe input script is the only part of a spend that the spender controls entirely, and it is public once it is broadcast. Everything in it, including a public key that was hidden behind a hash until now, is visible to everybody. A signature, once published, proves nothing further about the key but also reveals nothing that helps an attacker, provided the key was produced and used correctly; a key that is revealed and then reused, by contrast, loses the protection of the hash, a point on which the discussion of address reuse returns." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#InputScript> .
chx:S_StackExecution a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Stack, opcodes and script evaluation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Stack, opcodes and script evaluation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 44 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Scripts ;
    skos:definition "A stack is a list of items to which items are added and from which items are removed at one end only, which is called the top. The script machine of Bitcoin evaluates a script on a stack. Data is placed on top of the stack; an operation code, abbreviated opcode, is a one-byte instruction that, when it is encountered, uses items from the stack, starting with the topmost items (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Evaluation of the combined input and output script ends with a result: the script passes if there is a nonzero item on top of the stack at the end of evaluation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe design is simple on purpose. A machine with one stack and a short list of operations is easy to specify exactly, which a consensus system requires, because every node must reach the same verdict for every transaction. If all the scripts of a transaction pass, and all the other details of the transaction are valid, then full nodes consider the transaction valid (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Anything that a node cannot evaluate in the same way as all others would split the network.\n\nEvery spend of every output is checked in this manner. The book walks through the two scripts of this chapter. In the pay to public key example, the signature is put on the stack and the public key on top of it; the opcode OP_CHECKSIG consumes two elements, starting with the public key and followed by the signature, verifies that the signature corresponds to the public key and commits to various fields of the transaction, and replaces itself on the stack with 1 if the signature is correct and with 0 if it is not (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe pay to public key hash example uses four more opcodes. OP_DUP duplicates the top item, so that the public key is on the stack twice. OP_HASH160 replaces the top item with its HASH160. The commitment that was stored in the output script is pushed. OP_EQUALVERIFY consumes the top two items and checks that they are equal, and if they are not, the whole script fails. What remains is the signature and the public key, which OP_CHECKSIG consumes as before (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's checks run exactly this sequence on a list used as a stack, with the real HASH160 of the example key: with the right commitment the signature and the key remain, with a wrong one the script fails; and they confirm that an ECDSA signature made by the toolkit's sketch verifies against the key and fails for a different message.\n\nThe book's text prints the output script of the public key hash scheme with the opcode OP_EQUAL, but the combined script printed a few lines later and the script that Bitcoin Core reports for such an address both use OP_EQUALVERIFY, whose value is 0x88 in the source code of Bitcoin Core while that of OP_EQUAL is 0x87 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The difference matters: OP_EQUAL leaves a truth value on the stack while OP_EQUALVERIFY stops the script if the items differ. The stack is also an abstraction of the machine, not a picture of the signature check: the book says only that the signature commits to various fields of the transaction, and this chapter does not reconstruct what is signed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#StackExecution> .
chx:S_Legacy a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Legacy addresses"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Legacy addresses" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 45 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Address ;
    skos:definition "Legacy addresses are the address kinds that Bitcoin used before the introduction of segregated witness, and the book still calls them legacy: pay to public key hash (P2PKH) and pay to script hash (P2SH), both written with the base58check encoding (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This level also describes the two forms of payment that came before them, the payment by IP address and the output that pays a public key directly, because they explain what the legacy kinds were designed to improve.\n\nThe legacy kinds solve the problem of communicating a public key by replacing it with a short commitment, a hash of the key, that a receiver can pass on safely. They also introduced a general principle, which later address kinds keep: the receiver chooses the script, the payer needs to know only a short fingerprint of it, and the script is revealed when the funds are spent. The book states that P2PKH and P2SH are the only two script templates used with base58check encoding, that they have become less common over time, and that they were supplanted by the bech32 family of addresses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nLegacy addresses remain in use and remain valid. They begin with the digit 1 or 3, they are accepted by current wallets such as Bitcoin Core, and a wallet that supports older addresses must continue to recognise them. A new wallet can generate them on request, though it uses a newer kind by default, a point that the section on the default address type returns to.\n\nThe concepts of the level follow history. The payment by IP address and the pay to public key output show the first scheme and its limits. The hash commitment, then the P2PKH address, show how a short fingerprint solved the problem. The redeem script, the multisignature script and the P2SH address show how the same idea was extended to arbitrary scripts. The three attacks on short hashes close the level, because they explain why the commitments of the next level are longer.\n\nLegacy does not mean unsafe, and it does not mean obsolete in the same sense as a paper wallet. The book recommends that all new wallets use newer types of address, but it says plainly that there is no immediate threat to anyone creating new P2SH addresses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The reason for the recommendation is cost and convenience, together with a theoretical risk, and a reader should learn to separate these three." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Legacy> .
chx:S_IpAddressPayment a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Payment to an IP address"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Payment to an IP address" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 46 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Legacy ;
    skos:definition "An IP address is the number that identifies a host on the Internet: the Internet Protocol moves blocks of data called datagrams from sources to destinations, where sources and destinations are hosts identified by fixed-length addresses, and the address is four octets, that is 32 bits, long (Postel, 1981). The earliest version of the Bitcoin software allowed a spender to enter the receiver's IP address instead of a public key, as the book's picture of an early send screen shows (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This was the first form of address, and it was removed later.\n\nThe scheme answered the problem of the long public key in the simplest possible way: the receiver does not hand over a key, only an address that is short enough to type. The book explains the mechanism. If Alice entered Bob's IP address in Bitcoin 0.1, her full node would establish a connection with his full node and receive a new public key from Bob's wallet that his node had never previously given anyone. That the key was new was important, because it ensured that different transactions paying Bob could not be connected together by someone looking at the blockchain and noticing that all of them paid the same public key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe scheme is of historical interest only. The book says that the feature was later removed, because there are many problems with using IP addresses, and that no widely used program has supported IP address payments for almost a decade (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is nonetheless useful to know, because later features of the Bitcoin protocol can be understood as answers to its defects.\n\nThe first defect is that the receiver needs the wallet to be online at its IP address, and to be accessible from the outside world. The book lists the ordinary reasons for which that is not the case: the computer is turned off at night, the laptop goes to sleep, there is a firewall in the way, or the receiver uses Network Address Translation (NAT) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Network Address Translation is a method by which IP addresses are mapped from one group to another, so that a network's internal addresses, which cannot be used outside the network, need not be (Srisuresh and Egevang, 2001). A receiver behind such a translation is not directly reachable by a stranger.\n\nThe lesson is not that the scheme was foolish but that its good idea, a fresh public key for every payment, survived while its means of delivery did not. Fresh keys reduce the linking of payments, and the later address kinds, together with wallets that derive many keys from one seed, achieve the same effect without requiring the receiver to be online. A reader should not conclude that Bitcoin addresses are IP addresses: no address of the later kinds contains one." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#IpAddressPayment> .
chx:S_P2pk a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Pay to public key (P2PK)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Pay to public key (P2PK)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 47 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Legacy ;
    skos:definition "Pay to public key, abbreviated P2PK, is the output type in which the output script consists of the receiver's public key followed by the opcode OP_CHECKSIG, and the spender's input script consists of nothing but the signature. The book writes the two scripts as <Bob's public key> OP_CHECKSIG and <Bob's signature> (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This is the form of the original paper's payment, with a script around it.\n\nP2PK is the baseline against which the later kinds are measured. It places the whole public key in the output, so that the output is as large as the key, and the key is visible to everyone as soon as the output exists. The book remarks that the script uses the same public key and signature described in the original paper but adds the complexity of two script fields and an opcode, and promises that the benefits will become visible in the next sections (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book states that this type of output is known today as pay to public key, that it was never widely used for payments, and that the payment by IP address that went with it has not been supported by any widely used program for almost a decade (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In practice a modern reader meets P2PK mainly in discussions of the early blockchain.\n\nThe evaluation is the one explained in the concept on the stack machine. The signature is put on the stack, then the public key is placed on top of it, and OP_CHECKSIG consumes the two and replaces them with 1 if the signature is correct, which leaves a nonzero item on top and lets the script pass. The chapter's checks apply the same stack operations to a list and obtain the single value 1, and they check with the toolkit's sketch of a signature that a signature made with the private key verifies against the public key.\n\nIn P2PK the public key sits in the output for ever, long before it is spent. That gives up the protection that a hash provides, and it makes the output as long as a public key, 65 bytes in the early encoding, against the 20 bytes of the commitment that the next concept introduces (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The size is also the reason why the receiver of a P2PK payment cannot easily communicate where to pay: the public key is a long string that people cannot dictate." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#P2pk> .
chx:S_Commitment a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Hash commitment (HASH160)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Hash commitment (HASH160)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 48 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Legacy ;
    skos:definition "A commitment is a value that a person publishes now in order to be bound to a hidden value that will be revealed later. A cryptographic hash function gives a simple commitment: its output is always the same for the same input, and a secure function also makes it impractical to find a different input that gives a previously seen output, so the output is a promise that, in practice, only input x will produce output X (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the legacy addresses the hidden value is the receiver's public key, and the commitment is its HASH160.\n\nThe commitment is the idea at the centre of the legacy addresses. The book explains it with a game. The question is in what year Satoshi Nakamoto started working on Bitcoin; the answerer gives a commitment to the answer in the form of the SHA256 hash 94d7a772612c8f2f2ec609d41f5bd3d04a5aa1dfe3582f04af517d396a302e4e, and later reveals the sentence and shows that hashing it gives the same output (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the same way Bob can give us a cryptographically secure commitment to his public key, and if he later reveals the key and we verify that it produces the same commitment, we can be sure that it was exactly the key used for the earlier commitment (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nCommitments are met throughout this chapter. The 20 bytes inside a P2PKH address commit to a public key, the 20 bytes inside a P2SH address commit to a redeem script, the 20 or 32 bytes of a witness program commit to a key or a script, and the 32-byte witness program of a taproot output is a point that may commit to additional data (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In every case the output holds only the short commitment, and the long object appears in the spending transaction, where nodes check it against the commitment.\n\nThe chapter's checks reproduce the book's example: the SHA256 of the sentence, with the line break that the shell command echo adds, is exactly the value that the book prints. For a public key the commitment is computed by HASH160, A = RIPEMD160(SHA256(K)), a 20-byte value, against the 65 bytes of the uncompressed public key of the early software (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The compressed key of the book's example commits to bbc1e42a39d05a4cc61752d6963b7f69d09bb27b, and the same key in the uncompressed form to 211b74ca4686f81efda5641767fc84ef16dafe0b, two different commitments to two encodings of one key.\n\nA commitment binds but does not necessarily hide. If the number of possible hidden values is small, anyone can compute the commitment of every candidate and compare, which is why the book's game would be broken if the answer could only be one of a few years. A public key is one of an enormous number of values, so a commitment to it does hide it until it is revealed; but after the key has been revealed in a spend, the commitment no longer protects it. The commitment is also only as strong as the hash: the following concepts on attacks explain what a 160-bit output can and cannot guarantee." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Commitment> .
chx:S_P2pkhAddress a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "P2PKH address"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "P2PKH address" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 49 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Legacy ;
    skos:definition "A pay to public key hash address, abbreviated P2PKH, is the legacy address in which the output script commits to the HASH160 of the receiver's public key. The output script is OP_DUP OP_HASH160 <commitment> OP_EQUALVERIFY OP_CHECKSIG and the spender's input script is the signature followed by the public key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The address is the base58check encoding of the version byte 0x00 followed by the 20-byte commitment, and it therefore begins with the digit 1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe scheme is convoluted compared with a bare public key, as the book admits, but it allows Alice's payment to Bob to contain only a 20-byte commitment to his public key instead of the key itself, which would have been 65 bytes in the original version of Bitcoin. That is a lot less data for Bob to communicate to Alice (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The commitment must still be copied without error, and the book introduces base58check encoding as the answer to that problem.\n\nFor the book's example private key the compressed public key gives the address 1J7mdg5rbQyUHENYdx39WVWK7fsLpEoXZy and the uncompressed public key gives 1424C2F4bC9JidNjjTUZCbUxv6Sa1Mt62x. The book does not print these two strings; the chapter's checks compute them from its key, and Bitcoin Core 31.1, given the two public keys in descriptors, returns the same two addresses and reports both as valid (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A single private key therefore stands behind two different legacy addresses, a fact that the book stresses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe construction takes four steps. Compute the SHA256 of the public key, compute the RIPEMD-160 of that, put the version byte 0x00 in front, and append the first four bytes of the double SHA256 of the result before writing it in base58. The leading zero byte becomes the character 1. When the address is decoded, the checksum is verified first, the version byte tells the kind, and the 20 bytes are inserted into the script template. The chapter's toolkit performs exactly these steps and its decoder returns the version 0 and the 20-byte commitment for the address.\n\nAn address is derived from a public key; it does not contain it, so it cannot be turned back into the key. The public key is revealed for the first time when the output is spent, and from that moment the protection of the hash is gone, which is one reason not to reuse an address. Another is that the two encodings of the key give two addresses, so a wallet that was told about only one of them will not find funds paid to the other (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). An address that fails its checksum is not an address at all; the wallet must refuse it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#P2pkhAddress> .
chx:S_RedeemScript a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Redeem script"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Redeem script" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 50 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Legacy ;
    skos:definition "A redeem script, also called a redemption script, is a script that an output commits to by means of its hash instead of containing it. The book describes the BIP16 upgrade of 2012, which allows an output script to commit to a redeem script; when Bob spends his bitcoins, his input script has to provide a redeem script that matches the commitment, and any data necessary to satisfy the redeem script, such as signatures (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The specification of the upgrade calls the same thing the redeemScript or the serialised script (Andresen, 2012).\n\nThe receiver, not the payer, ought to decide what conditions govern the spending of funds. The specification states the purpose in these words: to move the responsibility for supplying the conditions to redeem a transaction from the sender of the funds to the redeemer (Andresen, 2012). A script with several keys can be thousands of bytes long, and the book points out that this is not only thousands of bytes to communicate to Alice but thousands of bytes for which she would pay transaction fees each time she sends money to Bob; a short commitment avoids both (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nRedeem scripts are met inside P2SH outputs. The book's example is a script with two conditions, one signature from Bob's desktop wallet and one from a hardware signing device: <public key 1> OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY <public key 2> OP_CHECKSIG (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A hardware signing device, the book says elsewhere, is a device that securely stores private keys and signs transactions that a less secure program prepares and broadcasts; it only needs to hold keys and use them to generate signatures (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe commitment is made with the same mechanism as for a public key: RIPEMD160(SHA256(script)). When Bob spends, his input script contains the signatures followed by the redeem script as a single data element, in the order in which the opcodes will consume them. Full nodes verify that the serialised redeem script hashes to the same value as the commitment, replace it on the stack by its deserialised value, and execute the result (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The specification adds a limit: the redeem script is an ordinary data push, so it cannot be longer than 520 bytes, which allows at most 15 compressed public keys in a multisignature script (3 bytes plus 15 keys of 34 bytes make 513) (Andresen, 2012).\n\nThe redeem script is revealed only when the funds are spent, and in the meantime no one can tell what conditions the output carries. That is a privacy advantage and also a risk: a payer cannot check that the receiver chose a script that can in fact be satisfied. The hash must also be computed over the exact serialised bytes, since a script that differs in any byte has a different commitment." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#RedeemScript> .
chx:S_MultisigScript a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Multisignature script"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Multisignature script" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 51 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Legacy ;
    skos:definition "A multisignature script is a script that requires signatures made with more than one key before funds can be spent. The book's example requires two signatures, one from Bob's desktop wallet and one from a hardware signing device (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The usual way to describe such a rule is m of n: m signatures are needed out of n public keys that are named in the script. The specification of pay to script hash gives a script of this kind with the opcode OP_CHECKMULTISIG in its example, {2 [pubkey1] [pubkey2] [pubkey3] 3 OP_CHECKMULTISIG}, which needs two of three keys (Andresen, 2012).\n\nSplitting authority among keys protects against the loss or theft of any single one of them. In the book's example a thief who steals the desktop wallet still cannot spend without the hardware signing device, and with two out of three keys, as in the specification's example, the loss of any one key does not by itself lose the funds. The pay to script hash upgrade was designed so that the receiver can choose such a script without the payer having to know it (Andresen, 2012).\n\nThe book notes that pay to script hash is not necessarily the same as a multisignature transaction: a P2SH address most often represents a multisignature script, but it might also represent a script encoding other types of transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the specification the opcode OP_CHECKMULTISIG can itself accept up to 20 public keys, and when it is not immediately preceded by a small number it counts as 20 signature operations towards a block limit of 20,000 (Andresen, 2012).\n\nThe script lists the keys, and the spender's input script supplies the signatures in the order in which the opcodes consume them. A node executes the script by checking each supplied signature against the keys. In the book's two-key example the first opcode, OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY, is a check that makes the whole script fail if its signature is wrong: the script interpreter of Bitcoin Core performs the ordinary signature check, pushes the result and then, for this opcode only, removes the result if it is true and returns the error SCRIPT_ERR_CHECKSIGVERIFY if it is false, whereas the second opcode, OP_CHECKSIG, simply leaves its result on the stack (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The chapter's checks compute the size limit that the specification states for a P2SH multisignature script of compressed keys, 15 keys and 513 bytes.\n\nMultisignature is a rule about keys, not about people: if one person holds all the keys on one computer, nothing at all is gained, and if the keys are spread over devices that fail together, little is gained. It also enlarges the transaction, since each signature and each public key has to be shown when the output is spent, and the spender pays for those bytes. The limit on the size of a data push, which the previous paragraph computes, is the reason why such a script cannot name an unlimited number of keys. A last point concerns the collision attack of a following concept, which applies precisely to scripts that several parties create together, and which is the reason the newer address kinds commit to 32 bytes rather than 20." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#MultisigScript> .
chx:S_P2shAddress a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "P2SH address"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "P2SH address" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 52 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Legacy ;
    skos:definition "A pay to script hash address, abbreviated P2SH, is the legacy address in which the output script commits to the HASH160 of a redeem script. The output script has a fixed template, OP_HASH160 <commitment> OP_EQUAL, and the address is the base58check encoding of the version byte 5 followed by the 20-byte commitment, which makes it begin with the digit 3 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's example is 3F6i6kwkevjR7AsAd4te2YB2zZyASEm1HM.\n\nP2SH gave the receiver the freedom to choose any script without troubling the payer. The payer's wallet needs only to recognise the one template and to put the commitment into it, so a wallet that has never heard of multisignature scripts can still pay one (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The specification describes the benefit as allowing a sender to fund any arbitrary transaction, no matter how complicated, using a fixed-length 20-byte hash that is short enough to scan from a QR code or easily copied and pasted (Andresen, 2012).\n\nThe book dates the upgrade to 2012, and the specification gives the details: it was a soft fork, it applied to blocks with timestamps from 1 April 2012 (1333238400 in Unix time), and miners signalled their support by putting the string /P2SH/ in the coinbase transaction of their blocks, that is in the first transaction of a block, the one that pays the block subsidy of Chapter 1 (Andresen, 2012). P2SH addresses are still in use, notably for multisignature scripts and for the nested form of segregated witness that the next branch describes. Bitcoin Core 31.1 validates the book's example address and reports it as a script address (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nTo spend, Bob's input script supplies the signatures and the serialised redeem script. Nodes first run the ordinary check, hashing the redeem script and comparing it with the commitment, and then run the redeem script on the remaining stack as if it were the output script (Andresen, 2012). The chapter's checks decode the book's address and obtain the version 5 and a 20-byte hash, and they find that Bitcoin Core's script for the address begins with a9 and ends with 87, the codes of OP_HASH160 and OP_EQUAL.\n\nThe template must be followed exactly. The book's warning is that when using P2SH one must use the specific template with no extra data or conditions, because if the output script is not exactly OP_HASH160 followed by 20 bytes and OP_EQUAL the redeem script will not be used, and any bitcoins may be unspendable or spendable by anyone (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Finally, P2PKH and P2SH are the only two script templates used with base58check encoding, and the book recommends newer address types for new wallets (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#P2shAddress> .
chx:S_PreimageAttack a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Preimage attack"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Preimage attack" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 53 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Legacy ;
    skos:definition "A preimage attack is an attempt to find an input that produces a given output of a hash function, that is, to open a commitment whose hidden value is unknown to the attacker. In Bitcoin terms, it means finding a public key or a script whose hash equals the commitment in an existing output. The book explains the danger: if the attacker finds the input in the same way as the original user did, they will know the user's private key and be able to spend the user's bitcoins (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe attack gives a way to measure the safety of a short commitment. For a secure hash function of 160 bits such as HASH160 the book states the probability as 1 in 2 to the 160 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), which is a number with 49 digits. An attacker's chance of finding an input by trying is proportional to the number of attempts divided by that number, so the only way to make the attack practical would be a weakness in the function itself.\n\nThe attack is the one that people usually have in mind when they ask whether somebody can guess their address's key. The commitments of the address kinds of this chapter are protected against it by the size of the hash output: the legacy kinds and the witness public key hash kind by 160 bits, the witness script hash kind by 256 bits (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), and a taproot output holds a point and no hash at all. It is also what the commitment game of the book relies on: the answerer cannot change the answer after the hash is published, nor can the questioner reconstruct the answer from the hash unless the set of possible answers is small (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe cost grows by a factor of two for every added bit. The chapter's checks print 2 to the 160 in decimal, with its 49 digits, and compare it with the 2 to the 80 hashes per hour that the book attributes to all Bitcoin miners combined in early 2023: even the whole network would need 2 to the 80 hours at that rate, which is more than 10 to the 20 years. The comparison uses the book's figure; the miners run a different hash function, so their hardware cannot be turned to this task (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nResistance to a preimage attack is the strongest of the three hash properties that this chapter uses, and it is not the one in danger. The attacks that justify the book's recommendation to use newer addresses are the second preimage attack and the collision attack, in which the attacker has more freedom. A reader should not take the safety of a preimage attack as a statement about the safety of the whole scheme." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#PreimageAttack> .
chx:S_SecondPreimageAttack a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Second preimage attack"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Second preimage attack" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 54 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Legacy ;
    skos:definition "A second preimage attack is an attempt to find, for a given input, a different input that produces the same hash output. The book considers the attacker who tries to generate two different inputs, for example two redeem scripts, that produce the same commitment, and states that for addresses created entirely by a single party the chance of generating a different input for an existing commitment is also about 1 in 2 to the 160 for the HASH160 algorithm (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe attack matters because a commitment is supposed to bind its author to one value. If a second input with the same hash could be found for a given first one, then the author of the commitment would not be bound by it, and an output committing to a script could be spent with another script. For addresses made by one honest party the attack is as hard as the preimage attack, which is why P2SH is considered safe in that case.\n\nThe distinction between the cases is the one made in the book's box about P2SH collision attacks. When all the inputs are produced by a single party that acts honestly, the attack is a second preimage attack, with a probability of 1 in 2 to the 160. When the attacker takes part in producing the input, the situation is different, as the next concept explains (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe estimate can be reasoned in one line. A hash output has 2 to the 160 possible values; for one fixed input, a random different input hits the same value with probability one in that number. The chapter's checks show that the figure equals that of the preimage attack. The case is exactly like the preimage attack for an attacker who sees only the commitment, and the main difference is the starting knowledge: the attacker has the original input, but has no more help in finding a second one.\n\nThe two attacks are often confused. In a preimage attack the attacker knows only the output; in a second preimage attack the attacker also knows one input; and in a collision attack, described next, the attacker may choose both inputs. The first two cost about 2 to the 160 hash computations for HASH160; the third costs about the square root of that, and that is the whole reason why attention is paid to who creates the inputs." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#SecondPreimageAttack> .
chx:S_CollisionAttack a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Collision attack"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Collision attack" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 55 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Legacy ;
    skos:definition "A collision attack is an attempt to find two different inputs that produce the same hash output, with no requirement that either of them be fixed in advance. The book applies it to P2SH: all addresses based on hash functions are theoretically vulnerable, but the position changes when an attacker is able to influence the original input value, for example when the attacker takes part in the creation of a multisignature script and does not need to submit their public key until after they have learned all the others (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In that case the strength of the hash algorithm is reduced to its square root, and for HASH160 the probability becomes 1 in 2 to the 80 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe attack matters because the freedom to choose both inputs makes the work far smaller. The attacker prepares two scripts, one honest-looking and one that sends the money to the attacker, such that both have the same hash. The honest partners commit to the first; later the attacker spends with the second. The book explains why the case is realistic: there are organisations that expect to receive billions of dollars in bitcoins to addresses generated by processes involving multiple parties, and that could make the attack profitable (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book gives a sense of scale. As of early 2023, it says, all Bitcoin miners combined execute about 2 to the 80 hash functions every hour; they run a different hash function than HASH160, so their existing hardware cannot mount a collision attack on it, but the existence of the network proves that collision attacks against 160-bit functions are practical (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's checks recompute the book's comparison and find that 2 to the 128 hash operations, the strength of the newer commitments, would take that network about 32 billion years.\n\nThe square-root effect comes from the birthday problem: among a number of random values, a match between two of them appears far sooner than one would think, once about the square root of the number of possible values has been generated. The chapter's checks show it on a hash cut down to its first 32 bits, which has 2 to the 32 possible values: hashing the numbers 0, 1, 2 and so on, the first repeated value appears at the number 95303, close to the 2 to the 16, that is 65536, that the square root predicts, and not near the four billion that a search for a given value would need. A secure 256-bit hash therefore offers 128 bits of collision resistance, and a 160-bit hash only 80. The book states the remedy plainly: there are well-established cryptographic protocols for preventing collision attacks, but a simple solution that requires no special knowledge on the part of wallet developers is to use a stronger hash function, and later upgrades to Bitcoin made that possible, so that newer addresses provide at least 128 bits of collision resistance (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book's figures date from early 2023. A third party's estimate of how many hash computations the whole network performs, read on 28 September 2026 for this chapter, gives about 2.8 times the book's figure for an hour, and with that number 2 to the 128 operations would take about 11 billion years instead of 32 billion (Mempool Space, 2026). The conclusion does not change: the book shows that 2 to the 80 operations are within reach of an operation the size of the Bitcoin network, and 2 to the 128 are not. The book adds that it does not believe there is an immediate threat to anyone creating new P2SH addresses, and recommends newer address types so that collision attacks need not be a concern (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#CollisionAttack> .
chx:S_Segwit a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Segwit addresses"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Segwit addresses" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 56 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Address ;
    skos:definition "Segwit addresses are the addresses written for segregated witness outputs. They are the answer of the Bitcoin developers to a practical question that the legacy addresses could not answer: how can a payer be given a short, easily typed string that works for the new kinds of output of a protocol upgrade and for kinds that have not yet been invented. The book presents two closely related formats, bech32 for the first version of the upgrade and bech32m for later versions, and says that bech32m is the current recommended address format for Bitcoin wallets (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe upgrade in question, called segregated witness or segwit, was introduced in 2017 according to the book, and, when it is used, it prevents transaction identifiers from being changed without the consent of the spender; it also provided additional capacity for transaction data in blocks and several other benefits (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Users who wanted direct access to its benefits had to accept payments to new output scripts, and that demanded a new way of telling payers what to pay. The book lists four problems of the older base58check addresses that the designers wanted to solve at the same time, among them the difficulty of reading them aloud and the need to upgrade every payer's wallet whenever a new protocol feature appeared (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA segwit address is what a current wallet shows by default. Bitcoin Core 31.1, for instance, gives out a bech32 address for a new wallet unless it is asked for another kind, and the addresses begin with bc1 on the main network (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Because the formats include their own version number, a wallet that can pay a bech32m address can in principle pay outputs of future versions that nobody has defined yet, which is one of the aims of the design.\n\nThe level has three groups of concepts. The upgrade itself and its nested form inside a P2SH address come first. The format is described next, piece by piece: the bech32 address, the witness version, the witness program, the human-readable part, the BCH checksum and the way errors are detected, the weakness of bech32 that was found in practice, and bech32m which repairs it. The three witness output kinds, the public key hash, the script hash and taproot, close the level, because each is a different use of the witness program.\n\nA new address format does not make old ones invalid, and a valid segwit address does not prove that the receiver can spend what arrives. The wallet that pays also needs to be able to recognise the kind: a payer that is not upgraded cannot pay a bech32 address, and the book explains that this need for upgrades was one of the problems the designers wanted to reduce (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The remaining danger lies in implementation errors, which is why the book asks implementers to use the published test vectors." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Segwit> .
chx:S_SegwitUpgrade a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Segregated witness upgrade"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Segregated witness upgrade" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 57 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "Segregated witness, shortened to segwit, is an upgrade of the Bitcoin protocol that defines a new structure called the witness, committed to blocks separately from the tree of transactions. The structure contains the data required to check that a transaction is valid, but not the data that determines what the transaction does; in particular, scripts and signatures are moved into it (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The book dates the upgrade to 2017 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), and the specification, BIP141, has the status Deployed (Lombrozo et al., 2015). Chapter 3 introduced the same upgrade from the side of the node, listing segregated witness and the transaction identifiers among the data that a node handles.\n\nThe first motivation that the specification gives is the end of unintentional malleability. Because signature data is no longer part of the transaction hash, changes to how the transaction was signed are no longer relevant to transaction identification (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The book describes the same benefit: when the upgrade is used, the transaction identifier cannot be changed without the consent of the spending user, or of the quorum of signers when several signatures are required (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Transaction identifiers that are stable are a prerequisite for chains of unconfirmed transactions, and the specification mentions that this is important for off-chain protocols such as the Lightning Network (Lombrozo et al., 2015).\n\nSegwit is met whenever an address begins with bc1, and it is the reason why a transaction has two identifiers. The txid remains the double SHA256 of the traditional serialisation, while a new identifier, the wtxid, is the double SHA256 of the serialisation that includes the witness (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The worked example shows the consequence on two short byte strings: hashing the bytes of a transaction without its witness and hashing the same bytes with the witness appended give two completely different identifiers, which is why the witness had to be left out of the first of them. The upgrade was designed to be a soft fork, compatible with nodes that did not upgrade, and it did not make older address kinds invalid; it also gave blocks additional capacity for transaction data, as the book says (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe specification sets out the mechanism. Each transaction input is associated with a witness field, a list of stack items, and for an input that does not spend a witness program the field is empty, a single zero byte. The witnesses of a block are committed in a second tree whose root is recorded in an output of the coinbase transaction, the first transaction of a block, so that the commitment is nested in the block's existing structure, which is what makes the change compatible with a soft fork (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The chapter's checks read the specification and confirm that it defines the two identifiers and the empty witness field in these words.\n\nA frequent error is to think that segwit hides signatures or removes them. They are transmitted and verified as before; they are only stored in a different place and not counted in the identifier. The specification states that transmission of signature data becomes optional for a peer that merely wants to check that a transaction exists (Lombrozo et al., 2015), and that is a saving, not a removal of verification. A second error is to think that using a segwit address makes a transaction private: the structure of the transaction remains visible." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#SegwitUpgrade> .
chx:S_NestedSegwit a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Segwit inside P2SH"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Segwit inside P2SH" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 58 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "Segwit inside P2SH, often called nested segwit, is the way in which a receiver can use segregated witness through an ordinary P2SH address. The redeem script that the address commits to is the segwit output script itself: the byte for version 0 followed by a push of the 20-byte hash of the public key. The book explains the idea: as the P2SH output type had the advantage that a spender does not need to know the details of the script the receiver uses, the segwit upgrade was designed to use that mechanism, allowing users to begin accessing many of its benefits immediately through a P2SH address (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nNested segwit was a bridge. A receiver wanting segwit did not have to wait for all payers to upgrade, because every wallet that could pay a P2SH address could pay this one. The book adds the limit of the bridge: to gain access to all the benefits, Bob would need Alice's wallet to pay him with a different type of script, and that would require Alice's wallet to upgrade to support the new scripts (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The bech32 address was the way out of the dilemma.\n\nThe nested form is still visible in Bitcoin Core, whose address type options are legacy, p2sh-segwit, bech32 and bech32m (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The documentation of descriptors calls the corresponding output a P2SH-P2WPKH output and writes it sh(wpkh(KEY)) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Such an address begins with 3 like every P2SH address, so it cannot be told from a multisignature P2SH address by looking at it.\n\nThe chapter's checks build one. The compressed public key of the example gives the 20-byte hash bbc1e42a39d05a4cc61752d6963b7f69d09bb27b; the redeem script is the two bytes 00 and 14 followed by that hash; the P2SH address of this script is 3FyC6EYuxW22uj4CaEGjNCjxeg7gHyFeVv. Bitcoin Core 31.1 returns the same address for the descriptor sh(wpkh(KEY)) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The payer sees only a P2SH output and nothing of the witness.\n\nCompared with a native segwit address, the nested one costs more space: the specification gives the figures for the nested form of P2WPKH, an output script one byte bigger and an input script 23 bytes bigger than those of the native form (Lombrozo et al., 2015). It is also more roundabout, and the book's reasoning for the bech32 format is that it should lead directly to the new scripts (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The nested kind also carries the weaker 160-bit commitment of P2SH, a point discussed under the collision attack. It is a compatibility measure and should not be mistaken for a separate kind of key." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#NestedSegwit> .
chx:S_Bech32Address a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bech32 address"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bech32 address" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 59 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "A bech32 address is a segwit address written in the bech32 format, which is a checksummed base 32 format. The book gives its pronunciation, a soft ch as in besh thirty-two, and explains the name: bech stands for BCH, the initials of the three individuals who discovered the cyclic code in 1959 and 1960 on which the checksum is based, and 32 for the number of characters of the alphabet, as the 58 in base58check (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The designers' own explanation of the name is shorter: Bech contains the characters BCH, the error detection algorithm used, and sounds a bit like base (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nThe format answers the defects of base58check that the designers identified. It uses only numbers and a single case of letters, which makes it easier to read aloud and to copy; it can detect, and help to locate, errors; it can be written in capital letters, which takes less space in a QR code; and it has a mechanism that lets today's wallets pay outputs of future versions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book adds that despite its alphabet being almost half the size of the base58check alphabet, a bech32 address for P2WPKH is only slightly longer than the legacy address for an equivalent P2PKH script (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book's example, for a P2WPKH output, is bc1q9d3xa5gg45q2j39m9y32xzvygcgay4rgc6aaee, 42 characters long, and the book shows further examples for the other output kinds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bitcoin Core 31.1 reports the address as valid, as a witness address of version 0 whose program is 2b626ed108ad00a944bb2922a309844611d25468 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Version 0 addresses are always 42 or 62 characters long, as the specification says (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nAn address is built in three steps. The witness version and the witness program are rearranged from groups of 8 bits into groups of 5, each group is replaced by one of the 32 characters of the alphabet qpzry9x8gf2tvdw0s3jn54khce6mua7l, and the checksum, six more characters, is computed over the human-readable part and the data and appended (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). The chapter's checks do it with the toolkit, which reproduces the book's address from the 20 bytes of the program, and also with the reference implementation that the book itself uses; both give the same four addresses of the book. Decoding reverses the steps and fails if the checksum is wrong.\n\nThe format has a weakness that appeared only in practice, described in the concept on length extension. The alphabet omits the characters 1, b, i and o because they resemble others (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017), but the string is still easy to mistype in the middle of a long address. A last caution concerns case: a bech32 address must be written entirely in lowercase or entirely in uppercase, and a decoder must not accept a string that mixes the two (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Bech32Address> .
chx:S_WitnessVersion a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Witness version"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Witness version" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 60 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "The witness version is a number between 0 and 16 that tells which set of rules governs a witness output. In a bech32m address it is a single byte that is encoded as a single character immediately following the separator. The book says that the letter q is the encoding of 0 for segwit version 0, the initial version, where bech32 addresses were introduced, and the letter p is the encoding of 1 for segwit version 1, also called taproot, where bech32m began to be used (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). There are seventeen possible versions, and Bitcoin requires that the first byte of the data part decode to a number from 0 to 16 inclusive (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe version is the means by which the protocol can grow. A new kind of output receives a new version number, and wallets that can pay any version can pay it without a new address format. The designers' stated hope was that developers could build a wallet today that allows spending to a bech32 address and have that wallet remain able to pay bech32 addresses for users of new features added in future protocol upgrades (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). That is why the book asks implementers to pass the test vectors concerning the versions that are not yet defined.\n\nThe characters follow from the alphabet: q is the character at position 0 and p at position 1, and the book's last example, an address for the highest version, begins with bc1s because s is at position 16 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's checks look the positions up in the alphabet of the specification. In the output script the version is stored as an opcode: version 0 is OP_0, which uses the byte 0x00, but version 1 uses OP_1, which is the byte 0x51, and the versions 2 to 16 use 0x52 to 0x60 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA decoder takes the first value after the separator, checks that it is between 0 and 16, and uses it to decide which checksum constant and which program lengths are allowed: for version 0 the bech32 constant and a program of 20 or 32 bytes, for versions 1 to 16 the bech32m constant (Wuille, 2020). The chapter's checks compute the opcode bytes from the versions, 0x00 for 0 and 0x50 plus the version for 1 to 16, and obtain the book's values 0x51 and 0x60 for the first and last of them, with the value 0x60 matching the opcode OP_16 in the source of Bitcoin Core (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe book's warning is the most important thing to remember here: a witness version of 0 is OP_0, which uses the byte 0x00, but a witness version of 1 uses OP_1, the byte 0x51, and not 0x01 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A wallet that converts an address to an output script with the wrong byte creates an output that is likely to be unspendable or insecure, as the specification warns (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). Another mistake is to think that the version is part of the program: it is a separate field." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#WitnessVersion> .
chx:S_WitnessProgram a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Witness program"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Witness program" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 61 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "The witness program is the data part of a segwit output: the bytes that follow the witness version in the output script and in the address. It is between 2 and 40 bytes long. For segwit version 0 it must be either 20 or 32 bytes, and no other length is valid; for version 1 the only length defined at the time of the book is 32 bytes, though others may be defined later (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe program is the commitment on which the receiver and the payer agree. In version 0 the 20-byte program commits to a public key and the 32-byte program to a script, which means that the decoder can tell what kind of output is meant from the length alone. The specification of the address format requires decoders to enforce the known-length restrictions for the versions that have them, and quotes BIP141: if the version byte is 0 but the witness program is neither 20 nor 32 bytes, the script must fail (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nThe book's table shows the three kinds in use at the time of publication, together with a fourth for a future version: OP_0 and the 20-byte hash for P2WPKH, OP_0 and the 32-byte hash for P2WSH, OP_1 and the 32-byte point for P2TR, and OP_16 followed by two bytes of zero for a future example (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bitcoin Core reports the scripts for these addresses as 0014 and the program, 0020 and the program, 5120 and the program, and 60020000 (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nFrom the length rules the address lengths follow. A program of 20 bytes gives an address of 42 characters, and one of 32 bytes gives an address of 62; the book says that these are the two lengths of version 0 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In general an address is between 14 and 74 characters long, and its length modulo 8 cannot be 0, 3 or 5, as the specification derives from the rules (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). The chapter's checks compute the lengths from the bit counts and the smallest and largest case.\n\nThe program is not the address, nor is it the script: it is only the data that a template wraps. The same bytes are a public key hash in one kind and the start of a script hash in another only by their length and version. A decoder that accepts a program of an invalid length for its version produces an address that cannot be spent." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#WitnessProgram> .
chx:S_HumanReadablePart a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Human-readable part"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Human-readable part" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 62 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "The human-readable part, abbreviated HRP, is the beginning of a bech32 string: a short prefix of printable characters that conveys the type of data or anything else relevant to the reader. The book says that bech32m addresses start with it, and that for Bitcoin it needs to know only about the two already chosen: bc for Bitcoin mainnet and tb for Bitcoin testnet (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The specification allows 1 to 83 characters in general and leaves applications to restrict them further (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nThe prefix makes an address self-describing, and it keeps networks apart: an address written for one cannot be mistaken for the other, because the prefix is mixed into the checksum as well (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). The specification explains why the shorter bc was chosen and not btc: it is shorter; and tb has the same length as bc, which simplifies implementations' assumptions about lengths, yet is visually distinct (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nThe human-readable part is followed by the separator, the number 1. The book explains the choice: earlier proposals used a colon, but some operating systems and applications that allow a user to double-click a word to highlight it for copy and paste do not extend the highlighting past a colon, whereas a number does work, and 1 was chosen because bech32 strings do not otherwise use it, which prevents accidental transliteration between the number 1 and the lowercase letter l (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The specification adds that if the prefix itself contains a 1, the last one in the string is the separator (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nTo split an address, a decoder finds the last 1 in the string: everything before it is the prefix and everything after it is the data part, whose characters never include 1. The chapter's checks do so for the book's mainnet address and for the specification's testnet address for the same kind of output, and obtain bc and tb. In the checksum computation the high bits of each prefix character are processed first, then a zero, then the low bits, so that an error that changes a letter into another letter remains within the range for which the error-detection guarantee holds (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nChanging the prefix of a valid address does not give a valid address on the other network, because the checksum would no longer match; a tool that merely replaces bc by tb produces an invalid string. Nor should the prefix be confused with the version: the prefix names the network, while the character after the separator names the witness version." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#HumanReadablePart> .
chx:S_BchCode a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "BCH code"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "BCH code" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 63 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "A BCH code is an error-correcting code, named after the initials of the discoverers of the cyclic code on which it is based, and it is the mathematical heart of the bech32 checksum. The book says that the checksum is exactly six characters long and is created using a BCH code, a type of error correction code, although for Bitcoin addresses it is essential to use the checksum only for error detection and not for correction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The specification of the format describes the code as implementing a BCH code that guarantees the detection of any error affecting at most four characters (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nThe code gives bech32 a stronger guarantee than the checksum of base58check, which is only a truncated hash. A hash gives a probabilistic check; a BCH code can be designed so that a specific class of errors is detected with certainty. The specification states the proposition: any error affecting at most 4 characters is guaranteed to be detected, and there is less than a 1 in 10 to the 9 chance of failing to detect more errors (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nThe six characters of a bech32 address carry 30 bits, since each character holds 5 bits, and 2 to the 30 is just over a billion, which is the origin of the one-in-a-billion figure for errors beyond the guarantee. The checksum contains no information of its own (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). The specification includes the code that checks it, a short function that computes a polynomial remainder over the numbers of the characters with five constants, and states that valid strings must pass it (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nThe verification feeds the expanded prefix and the data characters through the function polymod, which combines each value into a running 30-bit state, and accepts the string if the final state equals a constant: 1 for bech32 and 0x2bc830a3 for bech32m (Wuille, 2020). The chapter's checks run the function on the book's address and accept it, then test the guarantee on that address. All 1,209 changes of one data character and all 712,101 changes of two data characters are detected, and so are all 100,000 random changes of three or four characters that the checks try.\n\nCorrection has a danger that detection does not. The specification warns that error correction erodes error detection, because correction changes invalid inputs into valid inputs, and if more than a few errors were made the valid input may not be the correct input; it therefore asks implementations not to correct beyond suggesting where in the string an error might be (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). Bitcoin Core follows the advice by pointing to the positions of the errors and nothing more (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#BchCode> .
chx:S_ErrorDetection a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Error detection"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Error detection" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 64 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "Error detection is the property of a format that lets software notice that a string has been altered by an accident, such as a mistyped character. The book states what bech32 gives: in an address of the expected length, it is mathematically guaranteed to detect any error affecting four characters or fewer, which is more reliable than base58check; for longer errors it fails to detect them less than one time in a billion, roughly the same reliability as base58check (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Beyond detection, for an address typed with just a few errors, it can tell the user where those errors occurred (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA wallet that can say where the mistake is makes the correction quick. The book describes the experience with base58check: if you accidentally transpose two characters your wallet will almost certainly warn that a mistake exists but will not help you find it, and it might take several frustrating minutes to discover the mistake (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For bech32 the decoder can mark the positions of the characters in error, which turns a rejection into a repair.\n\nThe book shows an address with two errors, whose wrong characters are underlined in its figure. Bitcoin Core 31.1 rejects the address with the message Invalid Bech32m checksum and reports the error locations 25 and 40, and the chapter's checks confirm that these are exactly the two positions in which the typed string differs from the correct address (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book obtained its figure from a decoder demonstration that localises errors (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe mechanism rests on the BCH code: the checksum is a remainder that equals a fixed constant for valid strings, so a few wrong characters change the remainder. One of the properties of these BCH codes is that they can be used for error correction, which is what lets a decoder suggest where an error is (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). The guarantee holds only if the length of the string entered equals the length of the original address (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's checks confirm the guarantee on the book's address by trying every single-character and every two-character substitution.\n\nThe guarantee has a condition that the book spells out: it applies only if the length of the address entered is the same as the length of the original. If characters are added or removed in transcription the guarantee does not apply and the wallet may spend funds to a wrong address (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This is the root of the weakness explained in the next concept. Detection is also not correction: the specification asks software never to apply a suggested correction without the user's confirmation (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#ErrorDetection> .
chx:S_LengthExtension a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bech32 length extension weakness"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bech32 length extension weakness" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 65 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "The length extension weakness is a flaw found in the bech32 checksum after the format had been designed: the guarantees about detecting errors apply only if the length of the entered address is the same as that of the original. The book explains that one of the constants of the algorithm happened to make it very easy to add or remove the letter q in the penultimate position of an address that ends with the letter p, and to do so more than once (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The specification of the repair says that whenever the final character is a p, inserting or deleting any number of q characters immediately preceding it does not invalidate the checksum (Wuille, 2020).\n\nIn version 0 the flaw is harmless in practice, because only two lengths of witness program are valid. The book works out the figures: the valid program lengths of 22 and 34 bytes of script correspond to addresses of 42 and 62 characters, so one would have to add or remove the letter q twenty times to send money to an invalid address without the wallet being able to detect it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For later versions it would become a problem, because developers did not want to constrain output sizes: without a constraint, adding or removing a single q could send money to an output that is unspendable or spendable by anyone (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book prints an intended address, bc1pqqqsq9txsqp, and five longer strings with a valid checksum. The chapter's checks verify the book's statement, by applying the checksum algorithm of bech32: all six strings carry a valid bech32 checksum, and none of them carries a valid bech32m checksum. Bitcoin Core 31.1 refuses all six when they are given as version 1 addresses (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The note in the specification of bech32m, that the flaw was reported in an issue of the repository of the reference implementation, shows how it was found (Wuille, 2020).\n\nThe lengths of the book's six strings show what has been inserted: the intended address has 15 characters and the five others have 18, 20, 22, 23 and 25, which means that three, five, seven, eight and ten letters q have been put in front of the final p. Because the string ends in that p, the checksum of bech32 stays valid after each of these insertions, and a decoder of that format cannot tell the six strings apart; the chapter's checks confirm the six lengths and that every one of the six passes the bech32 test. The repair, explained in the next concept, changes one constant of the algorithm and removes the flaw for insertions and deletions of up to five characters, as the book states (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe flaw does not affect segwit version 0 addresses or the funds that were sent to them, and it was fixed before it could harm users. The lesson for implementers is that a checksum has a design range, and that a property such as detecting any change of up to four characters says nothing about lengths. A further consequence is that wallets must reject addresses whose length is not valid for their version, which the specification's decoder does." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#LengthExtension> .
chx:S_Bech32mAddress a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bech32m address"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bech32m address" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 66 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "Bech32m, short for bech32 modified, is the version of bech32 with a single different constant. The checksum algorithm is the same, and in its final step the constant 0x2bc830a3 is combined by an exclusive-or operation with the value in place of the constant 1 of the original (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Wuille, 2020). Bech32m is used for segwit outputs of version 1 and later, while version 0 keeps bech32 (Wuille, 2020). The book recommends it as the current address format for Bitcoin wallets (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe aim was to remove the length extension weakness without constraining future output sizes. The book reports that developers analysed the bech32 problem exhaustively and found that changing a single constant would eliminate it, so that any insertion or deletion of up to five characters is missed less often than one time in a billion (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The specification explains why version 0 was not given the choice of both encodings: permitting both would reduce the error detection capabilities, making it equivalent to having only 29 bits of checksum (Wuille, 2020).\n\nAll the characters of a bech32 and a bech32m address for the same underlying data are identical except for the last six, the checksum (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's taproot example, bc1p9nh05ha8wrljf7ru236awm4t2x0d5ctkkywmu9sclnm4t0av2vgs4k3au7, is a bech32m address, and Bitcoin Core 31.1 validates it as a witness address of version 1 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A wallet needs to know which of the two versions is in use to validate the checksum, but the version inside the address makes that easy to find out (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nTo decode, a program computes the polynomial remainder once and compares it with both constants: 1 means bech32, 0x2bc830a3 means bech32m, anything else is an error. It then checks that the encoding matches the witness version, bech32 for version 0 and bech32m for the others (Wuille, 2020). The chapter's checks encode the same 20-byte program once with each constant and find that the two strings share all characters except the last six, and the worked example shows the same for the book's taproot program, where the two encodings differ only in their last six characters, q2p3eu against 4k3au7. They also encode the program of the book's taproot address with the bech32 constant and see that a decoder that applies the rule rejects the result.\n\nA bech32m address with a version 0 program is not valid, and a bech32 address with a version 1 program is not valid either; software that accepts both for either version loses part of the error detection. The BIP350 document states that a string can never be valid in both encodings, because two valid strings of equal length differ in at least three characters (Wuille, 2020). Implementers should use the test vectors of the specification, including those for versions that are not yet defined (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Bech32mAddress> .
chx:S_P2wpkh a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Pay to witness public key hash (P2WPKH)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Pay to witness public key hash (P2WPKH)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 67 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "Pay to witness public key hash, abbreviated P2WPKH, is the segwit counterpart of P2PKH. The output script is the witness version 0 followed by a 20-byte witness program, and for the book's example the script is OP_0 2b626ed108ad00a944bb2922a309844611d25468 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The witness program contains a commitment constructed in exactly the same way as the commitment for a P2PKH output: the public key is passed into SHA256, the 32-byte digest into RIPEMD-160, and the result is placed in the witness program (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe kind combines the short commitment of P2PKH with the benefits of the segwit upgrade, and it yields a bech32 address only slightly longer than the legacy one. Since the signature and the public key move into the witness, the transaction identifier no longer depends on them, which is the point of the upgrade (Lombrozo et al., 2015). A P2WPKH output is the form that Bitcoin Core hands out by default (Bitcoin Core, 2026), and the specification notes that it occupies 3 bytes less in the output script than the equivalent P2PKH output (Lombrozo et al., 2015).\n\nFor the book's example key the chapter's checks compute the program from the compressed public key, bbc1e42a39d05a4cc61752d6963b7f69d09bb27b, and obtain the address bc1qh0q7g23e6pdye3sh2ttfvwmld8gfhvnmmfxuck, the same as the descriptor wpkh(KEY) gives in Bitcoin Core 31.1 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The specification of the address format shows the standard example: the public key of the generator point gives the program 751e76e8199196d454941c45d1b3a323f1433bd6 and the address bc1qw508d6qejxtdg4y5r3zarvary0c5xw7kv8f3t4 (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nThe script is 22 bytes: one byte for the version 0, one for the length 20, and the 20 bytes of the commitment. A node that sees such an output recognises the pattern, and when the output is spent the spender supplies the signature and the public key in the witness, not in the input script; the witness must consist of exactly two items, a signature and a public key, and the HASH160 of the key must match the program (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The chapter's checks recompute the hash of the generator point's public key and confirm the specification's address.\n\nThe word hash in the name should not be read as an extra layer of secrecy: the commitment is only 160 bits and is subject to the collision attack of the legacy discussion, but for a key created by one party alone it is as strong as P2PKH. The kind carries one key only, and only compressed public keys are accepted in P2WPKH and P2WSH, so a key that was used in its uncompressed form with a legacy address cannot be used with this kind (Lombrozo et al., 2015); scripts with several keys need the next kind." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#P2wpkh> .
chx:S_P2wsh a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Pay to witness script hash (P2WSH)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Pay to witness script hash (P2WSH)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 68 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "Pay to witness script hash, abbreviated P2WSH, is the segwit counterpart of P2SH. The output script is the version 0 followed by a 32-byte witness program, which is the SHA256 digest of the script that will be revealed when the output is spent (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book stresses that the algorithm of P2SH is not used here: the script is passed into a SHA256 function and the 32-byte digest is used in the witness program, whereas P2SH hashed the SHA256 digest again with RIPEMD-160 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe longer commitment is the answer to the collision attack. The book notes that for P2SH the RIPEMD-160 step may not be secure in some cases, as the collision discussion explains, and that a consequence of using SHA256 alone is that the commitments are 32 bytes (256 bits) instead of 20 bytes (160 bits) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The newer addresses thereby provide at least 128 bits of collision resistance, as the book says (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book's example is bc1qvj9r9egtd7mu2gemy28kpf4zefq4ssqzdzzycj7zjhk4arpavfhsct5a3p, 62 characters long, with the program 648a32e50b6fb7c5233b228f60a6a2ca4158400268844c4bc295ed5e8c3d626f. The specification of bech32 gives a worked example too: for the script formed of a public key and OP_CHECKSIG the SHA256 digest is 1863143c14c5166804bd19203356da136c985678cd4d27a1b8c6329604903262 (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). Bitcoin Core 31.1 validates the book's address as a witness address of version 0 and reports its script as a 34-byte one (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nTo pay to a script the receiver computes the SHA256 of the serialised script and gives the digest to the payer in a bech32 address. To spend, the receiver provides the script in the witness, together with the data it needs, and nodes check that the script hashes to the program before running it (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The chapter's checks reproduce the specification's digest from its public key and the opcode OP_CHECKSIG, and the matching address.\n\nThe 32-byte program tells the node that it must be a script hash, but the script itself is not visible until the output is spent. As with P2SH, the payer cannot verify that the script can be satisfied. The stronger commitment protects against an attacker who helps to create the script, but it does not make a poorly designed script safe." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#P2wsh> .
chx:S_P2tr a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Pay to taproot (P2TR)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Pay to taproot (P2TR)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 69 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Segwit ;
    skos:definition "Pay to taproot, abbreviated P2TR, is the output kind of segwit version 1. For a P2TR output the witness program is a point on the secp256k1 curve. The book says that it may be a simple public key, but that in most cases it should be a public key that commits to some additional data, a commitment explained in a later chapter (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The output script is OP_1 followed by the 32-byte program, and the address uses bech32m (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nTaproot gives a single output the flexibility to be spent either by a signature from one key or by revealing conditions that were committed to in the key. The program is only 32 bytes because the signature standard that goes with it, the Schnorr signatures of BIP340, encodes public keys as 32 bytes, instead of the 33-byte compressed encodings that are common in Bitcoin today (Wuille et al., 2020). Signatures take a fixed 64 bytes in this scheme (Wuille et al., 2020).\n\nThe book's example program is 2ceefa5fa770ff24f87c5475d76eab519eda6176b11dbe1618fcf755bfac5311 and its address is bc1p9nh05ha8wrljf7ru236awm4t2x0d5ctkkywmu9sclnm4t0av2vgs4k3au7. Bitcoin Core 31.1 reports the address as valid, of witness version 1 with that program (Bitcoin Core, 2026). When the same key is given to Core as the internal key of a tr descriptor, the address differs, because the descriptor language defines the argument of tr as the internal key from which the output key is made, while a descriptor called rawtr takes the key as it is (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA 32-byte x coordinate stands for a point because the curve equation determines y up to its sign, and the Schnorr standard settles the sign by convention: the point with an even y coordinate is taken (Wuille et al., 2020). The chapter's checks confirm that the book's program is a valid x coordinate, by computing a square root of x cubed plus 7 and testing that it satisfies the equation, and that the toolkit's bech32m encoding of the program gives the book's address.\n\nA taproot address commits to a key, not to a hash of a key, so the key is visible in the output from the start; the benefit of a hash hiding the key until spending is traded for flexibility and smaller signatures. The book returns to the topic in a later chapter, and this chapter treats it only as an output kind that needs a bech32m address." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#P2tr> .
chx:S_Practice a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Practice"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Practice" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 70 ;
    skos:definition "This branch asks what a user or a developer should do with the material of the earlier branches today. The chapter ends with advanced keys and addresses, vanity addresses and paper wallets, and it surrounds them with remarks about how practice has changed since the first Bitcoin software. The branch collects those remarks together with the current behaviour of Bitcoin Core, which was run for this chapter, and with the way in which implementers can convince themselves that their address code is correct.\n\nA chapter of a book is a snapshot, and software moves on. The book says of private key formats that the information is mainly of interest to anyone needing compatibility with early Bitcoin wallets, because modern wallets generate all their keys from one seed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A student who learns only the formats of the early years may look for features that current software has removed. The branch therefore compares what the book says with what a recent release of Bitcoin Core does, and where they differ it says so.\n\nThe material is met by anyone who installs a wallet, restores a backup, imports an old key or writes software that pays addresses. Chapter 3 described how to obtain, verify and run Bitcoin Core and its interface; this chapter used a release of it, version 31.1, as an independent authority on addresses, by asking it to derive and to validate every address that the book prints.\n\nTwo levels organise the branch. The first, practice now, covers the default address type of a current wallet, the export of single keys, the protection of a key with a passphrase, the deterministic wallet that has replaced single keys, the privacy cost of reusing an address, the vanity address, the paper wallet and the hardware device that has replaced it. The second level, assurance, covers the test vectors of the standards and the habit of checking an implementation against independent software.\n\nPractice is not static. A statement of the form wallets do X is true of some wallets at some date; the dates attached to the sources in this chapter, a book of 2023 and software of 2026, are part of the information. Where a source and the software disagree, the software of the date is the better witness to what happens now, and the source the better witness to what its authors intended." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Practice> .
chx:S_Modern a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Practice now"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Practice now" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 71 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Practice ;
    skos:definition "Practice now means the behaviour of current wallets and the advice of the book's third edition. It covers which address a wallet gives out, whether a wallet still lets a user take a single key out of it, how keys are protected, and which of the curiosities of the early years, vanity addresses and paper wallets, are still sensible. The book is blunt about several of them, calling paper wallets obsolete and dangerous and observing that vanity addresses have almost entirely disappeared (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nLearning the current practice protects against mistakes that came from earlier habits. The old way of keeping bitcoins, one key per address, each backed up on paper or in a file, led to lost keys and to leaked keys. The modern way derives all the keys of a wallet from a single seed, which can be backed up once, and this changes what exporting a key, protecting a key and printing a key mean.\n\nThe eight concepts of the level move from the wallet outward. The default address type and the export of a single key say what current software offers. Passphrase protection, the deterministic wallet and address reuse explain the surrounding security and privacy ideas. The vanity address, the paper wallet and the hardware signing device are three ways of obtaining or keeping a key, one of them fashionable in the early years and abandoned, one obsolete, and one recommended.\n\nA recommendation in this level is the authors' judgement at a date, and the book frames them so: the paper wallet section is a warning rather than a procedure, and the section on key formats says in its own words that it is mainly of interest to anyone needing compatibility with early wallets. The reader should keep the facts apart from the opinions and should check the date of any advice before acting on it. The facts of this level are of two kinds, and they age differently: what a document says, which stays true of that document for ever, and what a program does, which is true of one release on one day." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Modern> .
chx:S_DefaultAddressType a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Default address type"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Default address type" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 72 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modern ;
    skos:definition "The default address type is the kind of address that a wallet gives out when the user does not ask for another. In Bitcoin Core it is controlled by the option addresstype, whose help text lists the possible values as legacy, p2sh-segwit, bech32 and bech32m, with the default bech32 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The command getnewaddress accepts the same four values as its address type argument, and its default is whatever the option sets (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA default shapes what most users do. The default of the reference implementation tells the whole ecosystem which address format is regarded as current, and the book supports the choice when it calls bech32m the current recommended address format for wallets (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Defaults also matter for compatibility: a payer's wallet must be able to pay whatever kind the receiver's wallet hands out.\n\nThe chapter's evidence is a run of Bitcoin Core 31.1 as an offline node with a new wallet. The default address was a bech32 address, a P2WPKH output of witness version 0; asking for the types legacy, p2sh-segwit, bech32 and bech32m produced a P2PKH address, a P2SH address of a P2WPKH script, a P2WPKH address, and a P2TR address of witness version 1 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The first three names correspond to the kinds of this chapter that carry a single key, and the fourth, as the name says, uses bech32m.\n\nThe wallet reports the kind of each address through its descriptor, the output descriptor of Chapter 3: wpkh for the default and the bech32 type, pkh for the legacy type, sh for the nested type, and tr for the bech32m type (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The chapter's checks read these facts from the saved output of the run and also test the book's claim about the version numbers, finding witness version 0 for the first two segwit kinds and 1 for the taproot kind.\n\nA default is not a rule. Another wallet may default to another kind, and the choice of the kind of address for a payment belongs to the receiver, who makes it known by the address itself; a payer never chooses it. A default also says nothing about which kinds a wallet can pay, which is a separate question and the one that decided the design of bech32. Note finally that the evidence here concerns one release on one date, release 31.1 as it was run for this chapter, while the book was printed in 2023 and describes the practice of that year." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#DefaultAddressType> .
chx:S_KeyExport a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Exporting a single key"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Exporting a single key" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 73 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modern ;
    skos:definition "Exporting a single key means taking one private key out of a wallet, usually as a wallet import format string, so that it can be put into another wallet. It was an everyday operation of the early wallets, which held independent keys, and Bitcoin Core offered the commands dumpprivkey to export a key and importprivkey to import one. The book says that almost no modern wallets support the ability to export or import an individual key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reason is the deterministic wallet. The book explains that if a user exports a single private key from such a wallet and an attacker obtains that key together with some nonprivate data about the wallet, the attacker can potentially derive any private key in the wallet, which allows the theft of all the funds; and that keys cannot be imported into deterministic wallets at all (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The operation that was routine for independent keys is dangerous and often impossible for derived ones.\n\nBitcoin Core followed the change. The release notes of version 30.0 state that BDB legacy wallets, that is the ones kept in a Berkeley DB file, can no longer be created or loaded and list among the removed legacy-only commands dumpprivkey, importprivkey, dumpwallet and importmulti (Bitcoin Core, 2026). In the chapter's run of release 31.1 the help for both dumpprivkey and importprivkey answered unknown command (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA key can still be brought in through the descriptor interface. The command importdescriptors imports descriptors, and the help text warns that it triggers a rescan of the blockchain and requires a new wallet backup (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A descriptor can contain a wallet import format string: given the descriptors pkh and wpkh with the book's WIF strings, Bitcoin Core returned the P2PKH addresses 1424C2F4bC9JidNjjTUZCbUxv6Sa1Mt62x for the uncompressed string and 1J7mdg5rbQyUHENYdx39WVWK7fsLpEoXZy for the compressed one, and the P2WPKH address bc1qh0q7g23e6pdye3sh2ttfvwmld8gfhvnmmfxuck, the addresses that the chapter computes by hand (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe course material that accompanies this chapter describes the earlier workflow: to ask the node to expose a private key one used the dumpprivkey command, and its example prints the address 1J7mdg5rbQyUHENYdx39WVWK7fsLpEoXZy from getnewaddress and then the compressed wallet import format string of that address from dumpprivkey (Altunel, 2021). That workflow no longer works in a current Bitcoin Core, though the first command is still present (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A key that has been exported, even by a legacy wallet that still allows it, is a copy of the secret and must be treated like the secret itself; the loss of control of the copy is the loss of control of the funds." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#KeyExport> .
chx:S_PassphraseProtection a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Passphrase-protected key"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Passphrase-protected key" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 74 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modern ;
    skos:definition "Passphrase protection is the encryption of a private key under a passphrase, a secret phrase chosen by the owner, so that the key can be stored or printed without being usable by whoever finds it. BIP38 specifies how: it proposes a method of encrypting and encoding a passphrase-protected private key record as a 58-character base58check string (Caldwell and Voisine, 2012). The record contains everything needed to reconstitute the private key except the passphrase.\n\nThe proposal was written for paper wallets and physical bitcoins. Its motivation is a two-factor arrangement, something you have plus something you know: a person holding the printed record cannot spend without the passphrase, and the passphrase can be sent through another channel, for instance the telephone, while the record goes by post (Caldwell and Voisine, 2012). The third edition of the book does not cover the format at all, whereas the course material that accompanies this chapter, written on the second edition, presents it: there BIP38 is described as a common standard for encrypting private keys with a passphrase and encoding them with base58check, so that they can be stored on backup media or transported between wallets, with the Advanced Encryption Standard named as the cipher (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe BIPs repository gives BIP38 the status Deployed, yet the summary of the comments on it reads Unanimously Discourage for implementation (Caldwell and Voisine, 2012). The two statements are compatible: the format exists and is in use, and those who commented advise against building it into new software. The format is therefore of interest for old paper wallets and encrypted keys that someone may still hold.\n\nThe encryption uses three tools. The passphrase is stretched into a key by scrypt, a key derivation function that is deliberately slow and memory-hungry in order to resist brute-force attacks. The 32-byte private key is then encrypted with AES, a widely used block cipher, in two halves of 16 bytes. The first four bytes of the double SHA256 of the Bitcoin address serve as a salt for scrypt and as a check that the passphrase was right (Caldwell and Voisine, 2012). The result is encoded in base58check with the 2-byte prefix 0x0142 for a record without EC multiplication, followed by 37 further bytes; the specification publishes the first and the last of the 2 to the 296 possible strings of each kind, and the chapter's checks reproduce the first, 6PRHv1jg1ytiE4kT2QtrUz8gEjMQghZDWg1FuxjdYDzjUkcJeGdFj9q9Vi, from the bytes 01 42 C0 and thirty-six zeros with the base58check procedure of the toolkit.\n\nA passphrase adds protection only if it is not guessable, and the scrypt step only slows a guesser down; it does not stop one. The extra factor also adds a new way to lose funds: forget the passphrase, and the key is gone. As the book asks readers to use a recovery code and a hardware signing device instead of printed keys, the format is mainly a matter of compatibility (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#PassphraseProtection> .
chx:S_DeterministicWallet a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Deterministic wallet and seed"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Deterministic wallet and seed" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 75 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modern ;
    skos:definition "A deterministic wallet is a wallet whose private keys are all derived from a single random value, called the seed, by a procedure that always gives the same results for the same seed. The book of this course shows the idea with a hash function: if the seed is hashed together with the numbers 0, 1 and 2, three seemingly random values come out, and using the same hash function with the same seed always gives the same values (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book says that a user of deterministic key generation can back up every key in the wallet by simply recording the seed and a reference to the algorithm used (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe earlier arrangement, in which a wallet generated independent keys, needed a new backup every time a new key was generated or imported. The fourth chapter's section on key formats says that later wallets began to use deterministic wallets, which only ever need to be backed up once for typical use on the blockchain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This is also why Chapter 1's recovery code can be a short list of words: it encodes the seed from which everything else follows.\n\nAlmost all current wallets are of this type. The consequences for this chapter are the ones that the book draws: a single exported key can endanger the others, keys cannot be imported, and a vanity address, which comes from a separate search, does not fit into the scheme unless the user backs up additional data for it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The more elaborate hierarchical form, with a tree of keys derived from one seed, is the subject of the next chapter.\n\nThe book's own demonstration reproduces exactly. The seed is the hexadecimal text f1cc3bc0 and so on, the derived values are the SHA256 of the text 'seed + i' with a line break, and the three results begin 50b18e0b, a965dbcd and 19580c97. The chapter's checks recompute them with the hash function of the standard library. Real wallets use a stronger and more structured derivation than a bare hash, but the principle is the same: the keys are a function of the seed, so protecting the seed protects the keys, and losing the seed loses them (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe seed is a single point of failure in both directions: whoever obtains it can generate every private key and steal all the funds, and whoever loses it loses them all. The book says exactly that of the seed and its recovery code: someone else who gets the seed can generate all of the private keys (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A reader should therefore keep the idea of the deterministic wallet apart from the idea of backup: derivation makes the backup short, but it does not make it safe." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#DeterministicWallet> .
chx:S_AddressReuse a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Address reuse and privacy"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Address reuse and privacy" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 76 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modern ;
    skos:definition "Address reuse is the practice of receiving more than one payment to the same address. Because all the transactions are public, every payment to an address is visible together with every other payment to it. The book makes the point about vanity addresses: using a vanity address to receive multiple payments to the same address creates a link between all of those payments (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe link matters for people other than the receiver. The book gives two small stories: Alice may want to donate anonymously, and Bob may not want his other customers to know that he gives discount pricing to the charity (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same reasoning stands behind the first design of Bitcoin, in which the receiver's node supplied a new public key that it had never previously given anyone, so that different payments to Bob could not be connected by someone looking at the blockchain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nWallets avoid the problem by giving a new address for every payment, which is cheap when all the keys derive from one seed. The default behaviour of Bitcoin Core's command getnewaddress is exactly that: a new address on every call (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Reuse is still met when a person publishes one address on a website or a poster, and with the vanity address, whose point is to be recognised.\n\nThe link is created by the observer, not by the protocol. Anyone can scan the blockchain for outputs to the same address and list the payments, the amounts and the dates. A fresh address splits the history into pieces that have nothing visible in common. The chapter's checks illustrate the principle with a set: three payments to one address give one distinct address, three payments to three addresses give three.\n\nA fresh address for each payment does not give anonymity by itself, and the book is careful in its claims: it says that the reuse of a vanity address reduces privacy, not that avoiding reuse guarantees it. Reuse may be acceptable when a link is wanted anyway, as the book remarks for a non-profit that reports its income and expenditure to a tax authority (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The public key behind an address is also exposed when the address is first spent from, which gives one more reason to treat each address as single-use." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#AddressReuse> .
chx:S_VanityAddress a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Vanity address"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Vanity address" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 77 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modern ;
    skos:definition "A vanity address is a valid Bitcoin address that contains a human-readable message, such as the address 1LoveBPzzD72PUXLzCkYAtGFYmK5vYNR33 of the book, whose first four base58 letters after the digit 1 spell Love (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Such addresses are not designed but searched for: the process picks a private key at random, derives the public key and the address, checks whether it matches the wanted pattern, and repeats billions of times until a match is found (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe attraction is recognition; the book's example is a children's charity that wants an address beginning 1Kids to publicise a fundraising drive. The cost is computing time. The book is clear that the result is no less and no more secure than any other address: the key that was found spends the funds like any key, it depends on the same elliptic curve cryptography and the same secure hash algorithm, and one cannot find the private key of a vanity address more easily than that of another (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book reports that vanity addresses were popular in the early years of Bitcoin but have almost entirely disappeared from use as of 2023, and it names two likely causes. Deterministic wallets make it impossible to use a vanity address unless the user backs up additional data for each one, and most such wallets do not allow the import of a key from a vanity generator. And address reuse, which a vanity address invites, reduces privacy (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A vanity pool is a service that lets those with fast hardware earn bitcoin searching for vanity addresses for others (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book counts the work. An address with the prefix 1Kids lies in a range of about 58 to the 29, that is 1.4 times 10 to the 51, addresses; each further character of the pattern makes the pattern 58 times rarer, so that the frequencies run 1 in 58, 1 in 3,364 and 1 in 195,000 for patterns of one, two and three characters after the 1, and 1 in 11 million for four (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's checks recompute these powers of 58 for one to eleven characters. They also run a real, small search, counting private keys upwards from 1: the first key whose compressed P2PKH address begins with 1Ki is the number 1666, and the first that begins with 1Kid is the number 8810. The number of tries depends on luck, and these are far below the averages.\n\nThe search is a brute-force one and it multiplies its cost by 58 for every character, so long patterns are out of reach: the book says that a pattern such as 1KidsCharity would take 2.5 million years on a desktop computer. Its table of times follows its own rule, half the frequency divided by 100,000 keys a second, up to patterns of seven characters, but for eight and more characters the book's times are about a third shorter than the rule gives, and its frequency for eleven characters, 23 quintillion, should be 25 quintillion according to the powers of 58, as the chapter's checks show. The examples that this chapter found, with keys 1666 and 8810, also give a warning: a key found by counting upwards from 1 is not secret at all, and a real search must draw its candidate keys at random." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#VanityAddress> .
chx:S_PaperWallet a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Paper wallet"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Paper wallet" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 78 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modern ;
    skos:definition "A paper wallet is a private key printed on paper, often together with the corresponding Bitcoin address for convenience, though this is not necessary, because the address can be derived from the private key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book shows a sample and describes many designs: some for gifts, with seasonal themes; some for storage in a bank vault with the key hidden by a scratch-off sticker or by tamper-proof foil; some with detachable stubs that allow several copies to be kept against fire or flood (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA wallet on paper was an answer to the question of how to keep a key away from computers that can be attacked. The book now declares the answer wrong. Paper wallets are an obsolete technology and dangerous for most users, there are many subtle pitfalls in generating them, not least that the generating code might be compromised with a back door, and many bitcoins have been stolen in this way; they are shown for informational purposes only and should not be used for storing bitcoin (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book recommends the alternative in the same warning: use a recovery code to back up the keys, possibly with a hardware signing device to store keys and sign transactions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A paper wallet is met mainly in old gifts and old backups, and the reader who finds one should treat the printed key like any other private key, in the format described by the wallet import format.\n\nA paper wallet has to be generated by software, which chooses the random number and prints the key; the worked example counts what then has to be printed and kept safe, namely 64 hexadecimal digits, which are the 32 bytes of the private key. The security of the paper therefore depends first on that software and on the randomness it used, the issue of the earlier concepts on randomness, and only afterwards on where the paper is kept. Two risks follow: the software is not trusted, and the paper, once read or photographed, is a complete copy of the secret.\n\nThe recommendation against paper wallets is a statement about generation and about the format of a single static key, and not about writing things on paper: the recovery code of a modern wallet is routinely written on paper, and the book proposes exactly this. The difference is that a recovery code can regenerate a whole wallet from a trustworthy device, and a paper wallet is one key produced by a program whose trustworthiness the owner cannot check." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#PaperWallet> .
chx:S_HardwareSigningDevice a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Hardware signing device"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Hardware signing device" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 79 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modern ;
    skos:definition "A hardware signing device is a small device whose only job is to hold private keys and to use them to generate signatures. The book of this course describes it in the chapter on wallets: Alice gives a hardware signing device, sometimes confusingly called a hardware wallet, the key tweaks she needs, the device derives the child private keys and signs the transaction, and it returns the signed transaction to the less secure frontend for broadcast to the network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA computer or phone runs a great deal of software of unknown quality, and a key that is held there can be stolen by it. Another chapter of the book states the idea in general terms: unlike a smartphone or desktop computer, a hardware signing device only needs to hold keys and use them to generate signatures, and without general-purpose software to compromise and with limited interfaces such devices can deliver strong security to nonexpert users (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It adds that they may become the predominant method of storing bitcoins (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThis chapter meets the device twice. The multisignature example of P2SH, a signature from Bob's desktop wallet and one from a hardware signing device, shows it as one of the keys of a script, and the warning against paper wallets recommends it, with a recovery code, as the replacement (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The device is therefore a place where keys are kept, and not a new kind of address.\n\nThe division of labour is the point. The frontend, which can be an ordinary program, builds the transaction and shows it; the device checks it, signs it with the key it keeps inside and sends back only the signature. The worked example names the three steps in order and asks whether the private key is among them, and it is not: preparing and broadcasting happen outside the device, signing inside it, and the key never appears in the list of things that cross the boundary. Since a signature does not reveal the private key, the key is never exposed to the frontend, however compromised the frontend is (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The device still needs the secret to be backed up, so a recovery code, as the book says, goes with it.\n\nA hardware signing device is not magic. It protects keys from software on the computer, not from loss, so the recovery code must still be kept, and not from the owner's own mistakes, such as signing a transaction that pays the wrong address. The name hardware wallet is called confusing by the book because the device does not hold coins; it holds keys (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#HardwareSigningDevice> .
chx:S_Assurance a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Assurance"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Assurance" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 80 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Practice ;
    skos:definition "Assurance is the confidence that an implementation does what the standard says. In a payment system an implementation error is not a minor bug: an address decoder that accepts a string it should reject, or encodes a program wrongly, sends money to a place from which it cannot be recovered. This level of the chapter covers the two means that the chapter itself uses to obtain assurance, the published test vectors of the address standards and the comparison of independent implementations.\n\nThe book asks directly for such assurance. When implementing bech32m encoding or decoding, it says, use the test vectors provided in BIP350, and make sure the code passes the vectors for paying future segwit versions that have not yet been defined, so that the software remains usable for many years (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The specification itself explains why: experiments on implementations of the earlier format found that many wallets and services did not support sending to higher-version outputs (Wuille, 2020).\n\nAssurance works by comparing a program against something that was not produced by that program. A test vector does this by fixing an input and the answer the standard requires, so that an implementation which disagrees is wrong by definition rather than by opinion; vectors that cover cases the implementer did not think of - strings that must be rejected, versions not yet defined - are the valuable ones, because they test the boundaries where an implementation silently guesses. A cross-check does it by running two implementations written by different people on the same input and comparing, which catches a shared misreading of the standard only when the two authors read it independently. Neither method proves a program correct: both are ways of raising the cost of being wrong, and the published record of wallets that could not pay the newer addresses is what that cost looks like when nobody pays it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Wuille, 2020).\n\nThe reader meets assurance in this course as a habit rather than a topic. Every figure in this chapter is recomputed before it is stated, and where a standard publishes a vector the computation is run against the vector; where an independent program is available, the answer is asked of it as well, which for this chapter meant putting the derivations to a real release of the reference implementation. The same habit appears in the chapter's own record of findings, where a statement of the book that did not survive the check is written down with what the sources actually say (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe caution is that passing the vectors is a floor and not a ceiling. A decoder can agree with every published example and still mishandle an input nobody published; an implementation can be right about the arithmetic and wrong about where it keeps the key. And a cross-check between two programs that share a library is not independent, however separate the programs look, which is worth remembering whenever two tools agree suspiciously quickly (Wuille, 2020)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Assurance> .
chx:S_TestVectors a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "BIP350 test vectors"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "BIP350 test vectors" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 81 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Assurance ;
    skos:definition "A test vector is an input together with the output that a correct implementation must give, published so that implementers can check their work. BIP350 publishes three groups: valid bech32m strings, invalid bech32m strings with the reason for the invalidity, and valid and invalid segwit addresses of versions 0 to 16, the valid ones with the output script that they translate to (Wuille, 2020). The chapter's checks count seven valid bech32m strings, eight valid segwit addresses and fifteen invalid ones.\n\nVectors capture the cases that implementers forget. The invalid list contains an address with the wrong human-readable part, a checksum of the wrong kind for the version, a version out of range, a program that is too short or too long, mixed case, and padding errors, each with the reason. The valid list contains addresses of the versions that did not yet exist, so that wallets are forward-compatible. The specification says that all higher versions of native segwit outputs should be recognised as valid recipients, while no wallet should ever create them (Wuille, 2020).\n\nThe book recommends the vectors twice, once for the encoding rules and once for the future versions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's own toolkit was run against them, and it accepted all eight valid addresses with the right scripts and rejected all fifteen invalid ones, as did the reference implementation that the book uses. This is the kind of evidence the book asks implementers to produce.\n\nA vector test is a loop. For each valid address the decoder must return the stated witness version and program, and re-encoding must give the same address in lowercase; for each invalid address it must refuse. The checks of this chapter read the vectors from the saved text of the specification, parse the groups and run the toolkit's decoder on each; they also confirm that none of the bech32m strings is accepted when read as bech32 (Wuille, 2020).\n\nPassing the vectors is necessary but not sufficient: they sample the space of inputs and do not cover it. A decoder that passes can still contain a bug for a case that none of the vectors tries, so vectors should be combined with the other checks of the next concept, and with careful reading of the specification." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#TestVectors> .
chx:S_IndependentCheck a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Cross-checking with independent software"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Cross-checking with independent software" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 82 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Assurance ;
    skos:definition "Cross-checking is the comparison of the results of independent implementations on the same inputs. If a toolkit written by one person and a program written by others, without sharing code, give the same answer for many inputs, the chance that both have the same error is much smaller than the chance that one has an error. The chapter applies the method to its main example: the private key of the book, with its public key, its two P2PKH addresses and its two WIF strings.\n\nThe method is the way in which trust is built in this field, by checking that several independent implementations agree and not by believing one. The book itself relies on it when it advises readers not to write their own code for random numbers, and the specifications publish reference implementations, so that others can compare. Where an implementation is for teaching, as the toolkit of this chapter is, the comparison is the only reason to believe its output.\n\nThree sources were compared. The toolkit of the chapter, written for reading in plain Python, computed the public key, the two addresses and the two WIF strings of the book's key. Bitcoin Core 31.1, given the public keys and the WIF strings in descriptors, returned the same two P2PKH addresses and a P2WPKH address, and validated every address that the chapter prints (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The reference implementation of bech32 that the book itself uses produced the same four segwit addresses as the toolkit.\n\nThe procedure is simple. Fix the inputs, in this case the 64 hexadecimal digits of the key; run each implementation; compare outputs exactly, character by character. Disagreement points to an error in at least one of them. The chapter's checks recompute the toolkit's outputs and compare them with the saved output of Bitcoin Core, and they check the error positions that Core reports against the positions at which the typed string differs from the correct address.\n\nAgreement shows that the results are the same, not that they are right: two programs can agree on a mistake if they share a source of error, such as a misreading of the same specification. The comparison also covers only the inputs tried. The chapter's differences from the book, such as the statement about the origin of secp256k1 and the figures of the vanity table, were found by comparing the book with primary sources, which is a cross-check of another kind." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#IndependentCheck> .
chx:S_Semantics a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Semantics"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Semantics" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 83 ;
    skos:definition "This branch links the chapter to the theme of the course on identity and semantics. A key pair, as the chapter has described it, is more than a means of paying: it is an identifier whose holder can prove control without asking anyone. That is the property around which the W3C's Decentralized Identifiers are designed, and the branch explains the connection and uses it to name the ideas of the chapter in terms that are used outside Bitcoin.\n\nBitcoin's keys are often discussed as money. Seen as identifiers, they raise questions about how a name for something can be created, recognised and proved without a central registry. Chapter 1 introduced the idea of decentralized identity in connection with Bitcoin addresses, and this branch says what precisely makes a key pair suitable for it: the holder of the private key can sign, anyone can verify, and nobody has to grant permission.\n\nThe branch has one level, identifiers, with three concepts. The first is the proof of control by a key, the cryptographic property from which the rest follows. The second is the uniform resource identifier, the kind of name that web standards use. The third is the decentralized identifier, a specific standard that combines a name of that kind with public keys.\n\nA resemblance is not an identity. Bitcoin addresses and decentralized identifiers share a design idea, but they are different things with different specifications, and no part of Bitcoin is a decentralized identifier method simply by being a key pair. There are methods that do use a blockchain, and there are methods that use none, and whether any of them uses Bitcoin is a question about that method and not about Bitcoin. The branch therefore states only what the two sets of sources say, keeps the two vocabularies apart, and leaves the comparison as a comparison." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Semantics> .
chx:S_Identifiers a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Identifiers"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Identifiers" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 84 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Semantics ;
    skos:definition "An identifier is a name that refers to a thing, so that statements about the thing can be made, stored and found. A public key can be used as such a name, because a key pair lets its holder show, by a signature, that a statement comes from the one who holds the matching private key. This level of the chapter gathers the concepts that connect key pairs to the identifier standards of the web.\n\nIdentifiers matter wherever data from different sources must be linked. In the semantic web, a thing is named by an identifier that is unique and that others can use. Most identifiers depend on a central authority, which assigns names and can withdraw them. A name that its holder controls by a key does not need such an authority, and the question that the standards pose is how to describe and discover the keys behind a name.\n\nThe ladder works by separating three questions that are easy to run together: what a name is, who may issue it, and how a claim made under it is checked. A uniform resource identifier answers the first by fixing a syntax, so that the same string denotes the same thing in every program that reads it. The usual answer to the second is an authority - a registry that assigns the name and can take it back - and the identifiers of this section replace that authority with a key: the name contains, or resolves to, a document naming the public keys whose holder controls it, so issuing the name needs nobody's permission and control is demonstrated rather than recorded. The third question is answered by a signature, which anyone holding the public key can check and nobody else can produce. A Bitcoin key pair supplies exactly that third piece and nothing more, which is why it maps onto one field of such a document - a verification method - rather than onto the identifier as a whole (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022) (Berners-Lee et al., 2005).\n\nThe reader meets this level wherever data from two systems has to be joined. Within this course it is met in the semantic branch of each chapter, where a concept, a source and a finding each carry an identifier so that a statement about one can be stored and found; outside it, in any catalogue that must refer to records it does not own. The identifiers that a key controls are met in practice in credential systems, where the holder of a name proves control at the moment of use instead of relying on a directory that may be offline or mistaken (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022) (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022).\n\nThe caution is that a key proves control and nothing else. It does not say who the controller is, that the controller is the person they claim to be, or that the key has not changed hands; a document naming keys is only as current as whoever last updated it. And Bitcoin's own identifiers are not of this family: an address is a commitment to a spending condition, not a name with a document behind it, and a learner who treats the two as the same thing will expect a resolution step that Bitcoin does not have (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Identifiers> .
chx:S_KeyControlProof a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Proof of control by a key"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Proof of control by a key" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 85 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Identifiers ;
    skos:definition "Proof of control by a key is the act of showing that one holds a private key without revealing it, by producing a signature that others can verify with the public key. The book describes the property: a signature can only be produced by someone with knowledge of the private key, yet anyone with the public key and the transaction can verify it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Nothing in the procedure requires the verifier to ask a third party.\n\nThis is what makes a key suitable as the basis of an identity that nobody grants. Ownership of bitcoins is exactly proof of control: a node accepts a spend because the signature verifies against the public key to which the funds were paid, not because anybody vouches for the spender. The identity of the spender is not needed; control is enough, which is the privacy aim with which the chapter began (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe property is met in every Bitcoin transaction, and outside Bitcoin in the login procedures and certificates that rest on public key cryptography. In the Decentralized Identifiers specification the same idea appears as the ability of the controller of an identifier to prove control over it without requiring permission from any other party (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022).\n\nTo prove control the holder signs a challenge: a message that the verifier chooses, so that an old signature cannot be replayed. The verifier checks the signature against the public key. The chapter's checks apply the toolkit's ECDSA sketch to the book's key: a signature of a message verifies against the public key, and the same signature does not verify against a different message or a different key. The sketch signs a message hash with a nonce that the caller supplies, which a real signer must not do.\n\nProof of control is not proof of identity. It shows that the prover holds the key, not who the prover is, and not that the key is the one the verifier meant: the binding of a key to a person or an organisation needs something further, such as a document from a trusted source. A key whose private part has been stolen proves control for the thief." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#KeyControlProof> .
chx:S_Uri a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Uniform resource identifier (URI)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Uniform resource identifier (URI)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 86 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Identifiers ;
    skos:definition "A Uniform Resource Identifier, abbreviated URI, is a compact sequence of characters that identifies an abstract or physical resource. The specification of URIs, RFC 3986, defines the generic syntax and a process for resolving URI references, and its grammar allows an implementation to parse the common components of a URI without knowing the requirements of every particular kind of identifier (Berners-Lee et al., 2005). A web address is a URI, and so is the payment request address of Chapter 2, whose scheme is bitcoin.\n\nA URI gives a thing a name that machines can handle, store, compare and send: the same string refers to the same resource wherever it appears. The semantic technologies of the course rest on this property, since statements are made about resources named by identifiers, and the DID standard chose to be a URI in order to fit into that world. Chapter 3 introduced the IRI, the variant of the URI that allows international characters.\n\nThe identifiers of the DID standard are URIs. The example of the specification is did:example:123456789abcdefghi: the scheme did, the name of a DID method, here example, and an identifier that is specific to the method (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022). In a DID URL, which is such an identifier followed by further parts written in the generic syntax, a path, a query and a fragment may follow, as RFC 3986 allows: for instance did:example:123456/path or did:example:123456?versionId=1 (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022).\n\nSplitting the example at the colons gives three parts, which the chapter's checks print: did, example and the method-specific identifier. The scheme says which specification governs the rest of the string, the method says how to find the document that belongs to it, and the last part is what the method uses to look it up. Software that knows nothing about the method can still parse the string into these pieces because of the generic syntax (Berners-Lee et al., 2005).\n\nA URI names a resource; it does not say that the resource exists or that anybody controls it. Two different strings may name the same resource, and a name that looks like a web address need not lead to a page. For a decentralized identifier the question of who controls the name is answered by the method and by the keys in the document, as the next concept explains." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#Uri> .
chx:S_DecentralizedIdentifier a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Decentralized identifier (DID)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Decentralized identifier (DID)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 87 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Identifiers ;
    skos:definition "A decentralized identifier, abbreviated DID, is a new type of identifier defined by a recommendation of the World Wide Web Consortium that enables verifiable, decentralised digital identity. A DID refers to any subject, such as a person, an organisation or a thing, as determined by the controller of the DID. DIDs are URIs that associate a DID subject with a DID document, which allows trustable interactions with that subject (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022).\n\nThe standard was designed so that its identifiers may be decoupled from centralised registries, identity providers and certificate authorities. While other parties might help in the discovery of information about a DID, the design enables the controller of a DID to prove control over it without requiring permission from any other party (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022). This is the property that a Bitcoin key pair also has, and it is why the course treats the two together.\n\nA DID document contains information associated with a DID. Each document can express cryptographic material, verification methods or services, which provide a set of mechanisms by which a DID controller can prove control of the DID (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022). A verification method is typically a public key that can authenticate or authorise interactions, and a cryptographic public key can be used with a digital signature to verify that the signer could use the associated private key (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022).\n\nThe specification defines the syntax of a DID, a data model, the core properties, serialised representations and operations, and the process of resolving a DID to the document that it represents; the steps of resolution are defined by the specification of each DID method (World Wide Web Consortium, 2022). The chapter's checks read the abstract and the terminology of the saved specification and confirm the sentences quoted here. A Bitcoin key pair maps to the idea in a simple way: the public key is the verification method, and a signature is the proof of control.\n\nA DID is not a Bitcoin address, and a Bitcoin address is not a DID; they only share the design idea. A DID is meaningful only together with a DID method that says how documents are created, resolved, updated and deactivated, and the method may use a blockchain or may not. The specification speaks of a controller who can prove control, and, as for any key-based scheme, a controller who loses the key loses the ability to prove it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch04#DecentralizedIdentifier> .
